{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011ChFZUhzBcQwwa24VkvCGE","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_018ffhVSFLaw9wcg9HdYwJpV","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/Users/nick/code/tiiny_router_hermes.hermes-plugin-package-12-docs-for-package-install/tiiny-router/SKILL.md"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2307,"cache_read_input_tokens":60227,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2307},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"95803e3d-040d-4401-b98c-4ca7b716b706","uuid":"fd749772-f2c3-43d3-8015-8618c75ae118","timestamp":"2026-11-21T05:58:38.485Z","request_id":"req_011ChFZUhrFQfQZGPrWcnEMY","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_018ffhVSFLaw9wcg9HdYwJpV":{"file_path":"/Users/nick/code/tiiny_router_hermes.hermes-plugin-package-12-docs-for-package-install/tiiny-router/SKILL.md"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_018ffhVSFLaw9wcg9HdYwJpV","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tname: tiiny-model-routing\n3\tdescription: \"Run delegated sub-agents on the Tiiny device. Chooses the model that fits each subtask, loads it, locks the device against other agents, supervises the child, verifies its work, and releases the device.\"\n4\tversion: 2.1.0\n5\tauthor: Gary Garcia (garciacionet-alt), Hermes Agent\n6\tlicense: MIT\n7\tplatforms: [linux, macos]\n8\tmetadata:\n9\t  hermes:\n10\t    tags: [tiiny, delegation, model-routing, local-inference, agents, locking, lifecycle, thinking]\n11\t---\n12\t\n13\t# Tiiny Model Routing\n14\t\n15\tLet a manager agent use the Tiiny appliance as the host for its sub-agents:\n16\tchoose the model that fits each subtask, load it, keep other agents off the\n17\tdevice while it works, supervise the child, check what it produced, and hand\n18\tthe device back.\n19\t\n20\tThe shape of it: **a frontier model on a paid plan supervises, and the device\n21\tdoes the work.** The paid model stays in the main loop — that is where\n22\tjudgement and conversation belong — and everything token-heavy goes down to\n23\tthe box on the LAN. The paid subscription stops paying for volume, and the\n24\tdiversity of the Model Store becomes available to every batch.\n25\t\n26\t## When to Use\n27\t\n28\t- A delegation batch mixes different kinds of work — a research sweep, a\n29\t  filesystem index, a code edit, a browser run, a summary.\n30\t- The subtasks are big enough that the paid provider's cost and rate limits\n31\t  matter.\n32\t- Another agent in the AMC might want the device at the same time, and the\n33\t  two need to be able to see that it is spoken for.\n34\t- You need an image and want the right image model rather than the default.\n35\t\n36\tDon't use for: a single-model deployment where every subtask is the same kind\n37\tof work (set `delegation.model` and stop — but still `ensure` that model is\n38\tresident first, because nothing else will), or non-Tiiny providers (the\n39\tmechanism is provider-agnostic, but the tier table is Tiiny-specific).\n40\t\n41\t**The lightweight path skips most of this skill.** When the batch needs one\n42\tknown model and no wave planning, the whole job is:\n43\t\n44\t```bash\n45\tpython3 scripts/tiiny_ctl.py ensure --model \"Qwen/Qwen3.6-35B-A3B\"\n46\t```\n47\t\n48\tthen delegate. `ensure` is idempotent and lock-aware, and it is what the\n49\tscheduled scan jobs run as their first step. Steps 2-5 and 9 below are for the\n50\tcase where the models are not yet known, or the batch spans several.\n51\t\n52\t## Prerequisites\n53\t\n54\t- A Tiiny device reachable on the LAN, with its bearer available as\n55\t  `TIINY_API_KEY` (Hermes keeps it in `~/.hermes/.env`; the controller finds\n56\t  it there, in the environment, or in the vault's credentials file).\n57\t- The `tiiny` CLI at `~/.local/tiiny/bin/tiiny` for the load and unload\n58\t  commands.\n59\t- **A logged-in CLI session.** The CLI is one path to the model lifecycle; the device's HTTP API is another.\n60\t  The device publishes a route map at `GET /openapi.json`, and it does **not**\n61\t  list the model lifecycle routes — but the routes work. `POST\n62\t  /api/v1/models/<id>/download` and `/start` and `/stop` are all\n63\t  live on the device, authorised with the box's bearer, and they need **no session**.\n64\t  `tiiny login` is interactive (email + code) and is a human step.\n65\t- The routing backend plugin installed (see [references/backend.md](references/backend.md)), and\n66\t  `delegation.backend: tiiny-router` set in the active profile's config.\n67\t  This skill ships inside the plugin directory and the backend registers\n68\t  it when Hermes loads the plugin, so it resolves as\n69\t  `tiiny-router:tiiny-model-routing`. There is no separate copy under\n70\t  `~/.hermes/skills/`.\n71\t- **Models already downloaded.** The skill never downloads anything. It\n72\t  chooses and loads from what is already on the device, and a model that is\n73\t  not downloaded cannot be loaded — the plan will say so and pick something\n74\t  else. Download models yourself with `tiiny download <model>`.\n75\t\n76\t## The two halves\n77\t\n78\t| | What it is | What it owns |\n79\t|---|---|---|\n80\t| **The router** (`plugin/`) | A delegation backend. Hermes calls it once per subtask. | Choosing the model for each subtask, and its reasoning mode. |\n81\t| **The controller** (`scripts/tiiny_ctl.py`) | A command-line tool the manager agent drives. | The plan, the lock, the load, the restore, the catalogue refresh. |\n82\t\n83\tThe split is not tidiness. The delegation plugin has exactly one hook —\n84\t`route_task`, called once per subtask *before* any child runs — and there is\n85\tno \"batch finished\" hook. A plugin therefore cannot know when it is safe to\n86\tevict a model, and cannot know whether a sibling agent is already using the\n87\tdevice. Loading has to be a step the manager agent takes and can see.\n88\t\n89\tThe two halves meet at one file, the **plan** (`~/.hermes/tiiny-plan.json`).\n90\tThe controller writes it when it plans the batch; the router reads it when it\n91\troutes each subtask. That is how a specialist the controller just loaded\n92\tbecomes the specialist the child actually runs on.\n93\t\n94\t## The lifecycle\n95\t\n96\t```\n97\t  1  evaluate     what kind of work is this?\n98\t  2  pick         which model fits it?\n99\t  3  fit          do the models for the whole batch fit in one NPU pool?\n100\t  4  reserve      is the device free, and is it ours for the duration?\n101\t  5  load         put the batch's models on the device\n102\t  6  plan         one wave at a time — the device serves one stream\n103\t  7  supervise    watch the child, do not lose the thread\n104\t  8  verify       check the artifact, not the summary\n105\t  9  release      put the device back and let other agents in\n106\t 10  refresh      check the Model Store for models worth adding\n107\t```\n108\t\n109\tEach step has a checkable completion criterion. Do not skip ahead — a skipped\n110\tstep here fails quietly, and a quiet failure on this device looks exactly\n111\tlike success.\n112\t\n113\t## Pointing the router at the mesh\n114\t\n115\tThe router is single-URL by design. Point that URL at the mesh and it\n116\tbecomes multi-target: residency and the chat request both come from the\n117\tgateway, which picks the box. Four changes, all in the plugin and the\n118\tHermes config — no mesh change:\n119\t\n120\t1. **URL** — `TIINY_BASE_URL` in `tiers.py` prefers `TIINY_MESH_URL` when\n121\t   set, then `TIINY_BASE_URL`, then the device hostname. Set\n122\t   `TIINY_MESH_URL=http://127.0.0.1:8767/v1` in `~/.hermes/.env`.\n123\t2. **Key** — the mesh authenticates with its **gateway key**\n124\t   (`~/.hermes/mesh/gateway_key`), and rejects device bearers with 401.\n125\t   `device_key()` must return the gateway key when the URL is the mesh,\n126\t   and the device key otherwise. Detect by URL (`127.0.0.1:8767`), not by\n127\t   guessing from the key's shape.\n128\t3. **Id normalisation** — `/v1/models` on the mesh returns qualified ids\n129\t   (`tiiny-ant/Qwen/Qwen3.6-35B-A3B`) plus a bare `model_id` field. The\n130\t   tier table holds bare ids. Record **both** in `resident_models()`, or\n131\t   every tier reads as \"not loaded\" and the router silently degrades to\n132\t   the generalist.\n133\t4. **Provider** — add a `tiinymesh` provider block (`api` =\n134\t   mesh URL, `key_env: TIINY_MESH_KEY`, `transport: chat_completions`) and\n135\t   set `delegation.provider: custom:tiinymesh`. The route config is passed\n136\t   through unchanged, so the provider in the delegation section is the\n137\t   provider the child runs on.\n138\t\n139\t**The mesh accepts bare ids for chat** — verified HTTP 200 — so chat needs\n140\tno id rewriting; only the residency match does. A downloaded-but-not-\n141\tresident model auto-loads on first request (~35–40s), so a slow first call\n142\tis not a failure.\n143\t\n144\t**Editing `~/.hermes/config.yaml` is blocked for the patch tool.** It is\n145\tsecurity-sensitive: the tool refuses. Edit it with a Python heredoc in\n146\t`terminal` (back up first) or use `hermes config set`, then validate with\n147\t`hermes config get delegation.provider` and `hermes config get\n148\tproviders.tiinymesh` — `yaml` is not importable from the system Python,\n149\tso do not validate by importing it.\n150\t\n151\t**Changes to `.env` and `config.yaml` are read at server start.** A\n152\trunning session keeps the old values and reports \"No API key for custom\n153\tprovider … not set for this profile\" even though `hermes config get`\n154\treturns the key. Restart the app after the edits; verify the whole path in\n155\ta fresh process first:\n156\t\n157\t```bash\n158\thermes -z \"Reply with exactly: MESHOK\" \\\n159\t  -m \"Qwen/Qwen3.6-35B-A3B\" --provider custom:tiinymesh\n160\t```\n161\t\n162\tA `MESHOK` answer proves provider resolution, key lookup and mesh chat. A\n163\tdelegation error in the already-running session afterwards is the stale\n164\tprocess, not a broken config.\n165\t\n166\t## Quick Reference\n167\t\n168\t### The tiers\n169\t\n170\t| Tier | Model | NPU | Context | Reasoning | On the device? |\n171\t|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n172\t| `research` | `InternScience/Sage-A1.5-Turbo` | 48% | 262144 | switch | downloaded |\n173\t| `research` (alt) | `endless-frontier/BigBang-v1-Turbo` | 54% | 131072 | switch | downloaded |\n174\t| `index` | `openai/gpt-oss-20b` | 27% | 131072 | **levels only** | **resident** |\n175\t| `code` | `Qwen/Qwen3.6-35B-A3B` | 47% | 262144 | switch | **resident** |\n176\t| `code` (alt) | `ornith-ai/Ornith-1.5-35B-A3B-Turbo` | 54% | 262144 | switch | downloaded |\n177\t| `code` (alt) | `Qwen/Qwen3-Coder-30B-A3B-Instruct-Turbo` | 55% | 65536 | none | downloaded |\n178\t| `code` (heavy) | `Qwen/Qwen3-Coder-Next` | 94% | 131072 | none | not downloaded |\n179\t| `browser` | `Qwen/Qwen3.8-27B` | 52% | 262144 | switch | downloaded |\n180\t| `draft` | `Qwen/Qwen3.5-9B` | 29% | 131072 | switch | downloaded |\n181\t| `general` | `Qwen/Qwen3.6-35B-A3B` | 47% | 262144 | switch | **resident** |\n182\t\n183\tThe Reasoning column is a snapshot of what the device reported on 2026-11-09.\n184\t**The device is the authority** — the router reads the capability at route\n185\ttime, so this table can go stale without breaking anything. See\n186\t[Thinking mode](#thinking-mode).\n187\t\n188\t### The commands\n189\t\n190\t| Command | What it does |\n191\t|---|---|\n192\t| `tiiny_ctl.py status` | Device, resident set with NPU shares, the lock, the hold, the plan |\n193\t| `tiiny_ctl.py plan --goal \"...\"` | Pick the models, check the NPU fit, write the plan |\n194\t| `tiiny_ctl.py reserve --owner NAME` | Take the cross-agent lock and announce it |\n195\t| `tiiny_ctl.py load [--wave N]` | Load the batch's models, evict what does not fit, verify |\n196\t| `tiiny_ctl.py ensure --model M` | Make a model resident on demand — run this before delegating |\n197\t| `tiiny_ctl.py release` | Restore the required set, clear the lock, the plan and the notice |\n198\t| `tiiny_ctl.py catalog --refresh` | Diff the live Model Store against the last snapshot |\n199\t| `tiiny_ctl.py image \"a job\"` | Pick the right image model for a job |\n200\t| `route_check.py \"a goal\"` | Read-only: which model would this goal route to, and why |\n201\t| `route_check.py --thinking off \"a goal\"` | The same, plus the reasoning state it would achieve |\n202\t\n203\t## Picking a model for a project role\n204\t\n205\tThe tiers answer one sub-task at a time. A project — a requirements doc to be\n206\tplanned and then run — has three roles (planner, manager, worker) and they are\n207\tnot chosen the same way: the planner is bought for depth per token, the manager\n208\tfor state retention across turns, the worker for specialisation. The decision\n209\ttable, the discriminators and the two-box topology are in\n210\t[references/project-role-selection.md](references/project-role-selection.md).\n211\t\n212\tTwo cases the tiers do not cover and that reference does:\n213\t\n214\t- **A manager is not a reasoning role.** It is a state-retention role, so\n215\t  thinking preservation and reliable tool calling beat raw reasoning depth.\n216\t- **A 100% NPU model is escalation, not a standing resident** — load it for\n217\t  the one hard decision and unload it, or nothing else fits beside it.\n218\t\n219\t## Procedure\n220\t\n221\t### 1 — Evaluate the job\n222\t\n223\tName the shape honestly. \"Index every file under X and classify each\" is an\n224\tindexing task; \"read these logs and tell me what broke\" is a drafting task. A\n225\tgoal that understates the work gets the generalist, which is the wrong model\n226\tfor it.\n227\t\n228\tThe router infers the shape from the goal text, and the `tier` parameter\n229\toverrides it. Use the override where inference would guess wrong — an\n230\tunknown tier is refused loudly rather than silently mapped.\n231\t\n232\t*Done when:* each goal states the operation, not just the subject.\n233\t\n234\t### 2 — Pick the model, and check it can run\n235\t\n236\t```bash\n237\tpython3 scripts/tiiny_ctl.py plan --owner \"$AGENT\" --task \"what this batch is for\" \\\n238\t  --goal \"index every file under the vault and classify each\" \\\n239\t  --goal \"research the latest on agent memory systems\"\n240\t```\n241\t\n242\tThe plan prints, per goal: the tier, the model, its NPU share, the wave it\n243\tbelongs to, and whether the model is resident or merely downloaded. A model\n244\tthat is **not downloaded** cannot be loaded — the plan falls back to a\n245\tcandidate that can, and says so.\n246\t\n247\t*Done when:* every goal has a model that is on the device.\n248\t\n249\t### 3 — Ensure the combination fits\n250\t\n251\tOne shared NPU pool, and a load is a **swap**: it evicts whatever was\n252\tresident. Models over 100% cannot be resident together — a 47% model plus a\n253\t54% model is 101%.\n254\t\n255\tThe plan packs the batch into **waves**: each wave's models fit inside 100%,\n256\tand a wave that does not fit with the rest is a second delegation call. The\n257\tplan also prints what the wave will displace and what stays alongside it.\n258\t\n259\t*Done when:* every wave's NPU sum is 100% or less.\n260\t\n261\t### 4 — Reserve the device\n262\t\n263\t```bash\n264\tpython3 scripts/tiiny_ctl.py reserve --owner \"$AGENT\" --task \"what this batch is for\"\n265\t```\n266\t\n267\tThis takes the lock and writes a notice into the vault's `hot.md` so other\n268\tagents in the AMC can see that Tiiny is spoken for. If the device is already\n269\theld, the reserve **refuses** and tells you who holds it and until when.\n270\t\n271\t*Done when:* the lock is held and the notice is in `hot.md`.\n272\t\n273\t### 5 — Load the batch's models\n274\t\n275\t```bash\n276\tpython3 scripts/tiiny_ctl.py load --wave 1     # then --wave 2, and so on\n277\t```\n278\t\n279\tThe controller writes the hold first — so `status` reports what the batch is\n280\tabout to have — then decides what to evict, then loads, then **verifies that\n281\tevery model in the wave is resident**. A model that did not land cannot\n282\tanswer — the device returns `404 \"is not loaded\"` and the child falls back\n283\tto the paid provider, invisibly.\n284\t\n285\t*Done when:* the load reports no model missing.\n286\t\n287\t### 6 — Plan the work: the device is single-threaded\n288\t\n289\t**Tiiny serves one generation-heavy stream at a time.** Two heavy children\n290\tstarve each other, and the failure mode is a *hang*, not an error — the\n291\tdevice keeps reporting healthy while it answers nothing.\n292\t\n293\tSo: run **one heavy child per wave**, not every wave's children at once. The\n294\tparent's own cloud route is a separate stream, so parent work and one device\n295\tchild genuinely run in parallel. If a batch needs a second workstream, give\n296\tit a named non-device provider.\n297\t\n298\t**A single `delegate_task` batch with two tasks is two heavy children.** The\n299\tbatch API is the easiest way to break this rule by accident: one call, two\n300\tentries, both generation-heavy, both pointed at the device. The symptom is\n301\tnot an error — it is a hang that reads like slow progress. One child sat\n302\t`waiting for model response` for over fifteen minutes while the device\n303\treported healthy, and its sibling stalled on the same pool. Dispatch the\n304\tsecond child only after the first has returned.\n305\t\n306\t**A hung child may still have written its output.** The hang is often in the\n307\thandover, not the work: the child can finish writing the artifact and then\n308\tstall trying to report it. When a child has run far past its expected\n309\tduration, **look for its file on disk before concluding it produced nothing**\n310\t— the artifact is the evidence, and the status line is not. Read what it\n311\twrote, then decide whether to keep it, merge it, or supersede it. Stop the\n312\tchild either way, so it is not still holding the device.\n313\t\n314\t*Done when:* no wave runs more than one generation-heavy child at a time.\n315\t\n316\t### 7 — Supervise the child\n317\t\n318\tThe child is running on a small local model, so give it a job it can finish:\n319\tstate the operation, the output, and where to write it. Ask it to save raw\n320\tfindings to a file early and keep its summary short — that is what keeps a\n321\tlocal model competitive with a frontier one.\n322\t\n323\tWatch for the two failure modes that look like progress: a child that is\n324\tstalled behind the device's queue, and a child that fell back to the paid\n325\tprovider. The child's route carries the backend and the label\n326\t(`code→generalist` means the specialist was not available).\n327\t\n328\t*Done when:* the child has written its findings at least once, and its route\n329\tlabel says what you expected.\n330\t\n331\t### 8 — Check the child's work\n332\t\n333\tA child's report is a self-report. Recompute the numbers it claims from the\n334\tprimary record with a script, and render what it built — a delivered HTML\n335\tfile is not done when it parses.\n336\t\n337\t*Done when:* a script recomputes every claimed count from the source and\n338\texits zero.\n339\t\n340\t### 9 — Release the device\n341\t\n342\t```bash\n343\tpython3 scripts/tiiny_ctl.py release\n344\t```\n345\t\n346\tThis restores the required models from `REQUIRED_MODELS` — the single copy of\n347\tthat policy, in `scripts/tiiny_ctl.py` (see \"The required set\") — clears the\n348\thold, clears the plan, releases the lock, and removes the notice from\n349\t`hot.md`. **Restoring is\n350\tpart of the run, not a follow-up**: a swap that is not restored breaks every\n351\tlater delegated child, and the failure looks like a model problem rather\n352\tthan a leftover state.\n353\t\n354\t*Done when:* the required models are resident, the hold is gone, the lock is\n355\tfree, and `hot.md` no longer carries the notice.\n356\t\n357\t### 10 — Keep the catalogue honest\n358\t\n359\t```bash\n360\tpython3 scripts/tiiny_ctl.py catalog --refresh\n361\t```\n362\t\n363\tThis diffs the live Model Store against the last snapshot and reports what is\n364\tnew, what is gone, and whose NPU share or context window moved. If a tier\n365\tmodel's figures changed, the tier table in `plugin/tiers.py` is now wrong —\n366\tupdate it, and update\n367\t[`tiiny-discovery/tiiny-model-catalogue.md`](../../Tiiny/model-catalogue.md)\n368\tto match.\n369\t\n370\tThe snapshot records **facts**; the analysis around them is written by hand.\n371\tNew models are worth adding to the tier table when they are genuinely better\n372\tat a shape the tiers do not cover — not because they are new.\n373\t\n374\tWhen you need a publication-ready catalogue with live `desc` + measured overlay, build it reproducibly: `curl -H \"Authorization: Bearer $TIINY_API_KEY\" http://tiinyhost.local/api/v1/models/online_models` to snapshot all 92, `cp app/data/overlay.yaml /tmp/overlay.yaml` first (the Hermes gateway refuses to open vault/iCloud paths directly — copy to `/tmp` before parsing), then merge `thinking` + `overlay.yaml` measured fields (`image_input`, `alpha`, `text_rendering`) via a script (see `/tmp/build_catalog_doc.py`) and write the MD to `~/SatanDocuments/` and a vault copy via `cp /tmp/*.md` — never hand-edit a drifted NPU/context cell.\n375\t\n376\t*Done when:* the diff is empty, or the differences are reflected in the tier\n377\ttable and the catalogue.\n378\t\n379\t## Thinking mode\n380\t\n381\tA reasoning model answers in two parts: the trace, and the answer. The trace\n382\tis not free, and on mechanical work it is not wanted. Indexing a folder does\n383\tnot need a chain of thought; it needs the folder.\n384\t\n385\tSo each task carries a `thinking` parameter:\n386\t\n387\t| Value | What happens |\n388\t|---|---|\n389\t| `auto` (default) | Nothing is applied. The model keeps its native behaviour. |\n390\t| `off` | Reasoning is turned off where the model can be turned off, and down to the model's cheapest reasoning level where it cannot. |\n391\t| `on` | Reasoning is turned on where the model can be turned on, and up to its most expensive level where it cannot. |\n392\t\n393\t```bash\n394\tpython3 scripts/route_check.py --thinking off \"index every file under the vault\"\n395\t```\n396\t\n397\t### The device knows more than a table does\n398\t\n399\tModels differ in *how* reasoning is controlled, and the differences are not\n400\tguessable:\n401\t\n402\t- Some models have a **switch** — `enable_thinking: false` stops the trace\n403\t  dead.\n404\t- Some models have only **levels** — `low`, `medium`, `high` — and their\n405\t  switch is ignored outright.\n406\t- Some models **do not reason at all**, and a request to turn reasoning on is\n407\t  not a thing that can happen.\n408\t- One model is flagged by the device as having no switch and honours it\n409\t  perfectly well. **The flag is a hint, not ground truth.**\n410\t\n411\tSo the router does not keep a table of this. It reads the device's own\n412\tcapability for the model it picked, at route time, and applies the control\n413\tthat model actually honours:\n414\t\n415\t```json\n416\t{\"thinking\": {\"supported\": true, \"toggleable\": false, \"levels\": [\"low\", \"medium\", \"high\"]}}\n417\t```\n418\t\n419\tThat comes from `GET /v1/models` for a resident model and\n420\t`GET /api/v1/models/online_models` for the whole store. The Model Store\n421\tchanges with every TiinyOS update, and a hand-maintained capability table\n422\tgoes stale quietly — which is worse than having none, because it is\n423\tbelieved.\n424\t\n425\t### \"Off\" means the cheapest available state\n426\t\n427\tFor a model with no working switch, `thinking: \"off\"` is **not a refusal and\n428\tnot a pretence**. It means the cheapest state the model offers: the lowest\n429\treasoning level it declares. The model still reasons — it just reasons less —\n430\tand the label says so rather than claiming a switch that does not exist.\n431\t\n432\t### The label reports what happened, not what was asked\n433\t\n434\tEvery route carries a label. The thinking state is part of it:\n435\t\n436\t| Label | Meaning |\n437\t|---|---|\n438\t| `[think:off]` | The switch worked. |\n439\t| `[think:off→low]` | The switch was ignored; the model is at its cheapest level. |\n440\t| `[think:on]` | The switch worked. |\n441\t| `[think:on→high]` | The switch was ignored; the model is at its most expensive level. |\n442\t| `[think:unsupported]` | The model does not reason at all. |\n443\t| `[think:unknown]` | The device would not say, so nothing was applied. |\n444\t| `[think:auto]` | Nothing was applied; native behaviour. |\n445\t\n446\tA route that asked for `off` and got `off→low` did not turn reasoning off.\n447\tSaying otherwise is how a token bill becomes a surprise.\n448\t\n449\t### Thinking on needs a token floor\n450\t\n451\tA reasoning model spends its budget on the trace first. With a small\n452\t`max_tokens`, the trace eats the budget and the request comes back **HTTP 200\n453\twith an empty answer** — a silent failure that looks like the model produced\n454\tnothing.\n455\t\n456\tSo the router applies an output floor (4096, the budget children already run\n457\twith) whenever a model's reasoning is in play. If you raise your own budget\n458\tabove that, raise the floor with it, or a reasoning child is quietly clamped\n459\tback down:\n460\t\n461\t```yaml\n462\tdelegation:\n463\t  backends:\n464\t    tiiny-router:\n465\t      thinking_max_tokens: 8192\n466\t```\n467\t\n468\t## The image benefit\n469\t\n470\tThe same device runs the image models, and picking the right one is the\n471\tdifference between an image that works and an afternoon. The controller picks\n472\tfrom what is on the device:\n473\t\n474\t```bash\n475\tpython3 scripts/tiiny_ctl.py image \"a realistic portrait of an engineer\"\n476\tpython3 scripts/tiiny_ctl.py image \"a logo with the word ARC in it\"\n477\tpython3 scripts/tiiny_ctl.py image \"a character turnaround, three views\"\n478\t```\n479\t\n480\t| The job | The model | NPU |\n481\t|---|---|---|\n482\t| Photorealism, legible text, fast iteration | `Tongyi-MAI/Z-Image-Turbo` | 26% |\n483\t| Maximum fidelity — fur, foliage, reflections | `HiDream-ai/HiDream-O1-Image-Dev` | 30% |\n484\t| A realistic person — skin, hair, fabric | `TaoBaiBai2025/Z-Image-Turbo-Creating-Realistic` | 26% |\n485\t| A character turnaround in one image | `YeiYeiArt/Z-Image-Turbo-CharacterDesignSheet` | 26% |\n486\t| Cut a subject out of an existing photo | `fal/FLUX.2-klein-4B-Background-Remove` | 26% |\n487\t\n488\tAn image model evicts the chat models — the NPU is one pool — so image work\n489\tbelongs inside a reserved batch, and the release puts everything back. See\n490\t[references/image-models.md](references/image-models.md) for the sizes that\n491\twork and the ones that do not.\n492\t\n493\t## The lock\n494\t\n495\tTwo agents delegating at once will fight over the same models and the same\n496\tsilicon. The lock makes that visible instead of silent.\n497\t\n498\t| | |\n499\t|---|---|\n500\t| **The mutex** | `~/.hermes/tiiny-lock.json`. Taken under `flock`, so two agents cannot both believe they hold it. It **expires**, so a crashed agent does not lock the device overnight. |\n501\t| **The announcement** | A marked block in the vault's `hot.md` — the AMC's own board of what is in flight — so agents that are not Hermes profiles can see the device is spoken for. |\n502\t\n503\t`status` shows both. `release` clears both.\n504\t\n505\t**The device cannot record who loaded what.** Its management API does expose\n506\tload and unload routes, but they report only the model and the outcome — no\n507\tactor and no owner — so there is still no server-side audit trail to read, and\n508\ta usage log has to be written on this side, by the tool that performs the load.\n509\tThat makes the controller the only writer that can be trusted: a log kept by\n510\tconvention is bypassed by every bare `tiiny load`, and a bypassed log is\n511\tworse than no log because it claims completeness it does not have.\n512\t\n513\t## The required set\n514\t\n515\t**There is no watchdog any more.** A five-minute cron poller used to reload a\n516\tfixed set of models whenever the device looked cold. It was retired for two\n517\treasons: that set was duplicated across three scripts and drifted apart three\n518\ttimes, and a fixed set is the wrong shape when a batch needs one specific\n519\tmodel.\n520\t\n521\t**Prove that, do not assume it — and do not trust a document that still says a\n522\twatchdog exists.** The four places a timer can hide, all of them cheap to read:\n523\t`crontab -l`; `~/Library/LaunchAgents`; `/Library/LaunchDaemons`; and the\n524\tHermes job store at `~/.hermes/cron/jobs.json` (there is one hourly job that\n525\tmentions \"watchdog\" — the Engraphis MCP watchdog — and it restarts a server,\n526\tnot a model). A vault `AGENTS.md` that describes a five-minute reload poller\n527\tfighting a deliberate swap is stale; the mechanism is gone, and quoting it back\n528\tto the user as a live risk is a wrong answer.\n529\t\n530\t**The mesh gateway has a restore path, and it is inert by design.**\n531\t`_restore_worker` in `mesh/gateway.py` fires **once at gateway start**, retries\n532\t6 × 20 s while the boxes come up, and only ever *loads* — there is no unload\n533\tcode and no timer. It is gated by `auto_restore` in `~/.hermes/mesh/config.json`,\n534\tand the log prints `[restore] disabled by config (auto_restore=false)` at every\n535\tstart. Note what that switch really controls: it turns off `keep_resident` too,\n536\tso on a box that declares one, a reboot leaves it cold until `ensure` runs.\n537\tThat is the deliberate trade — no mid-batch eviction, no unattended recovery —\n538\tand it should be stated as a trade rather than fixed by turning restore on.\n539\t\n540\t**What does evict a model mid-project is traffic, not time.** Two paths, both\n541\tin the mesh: the `borrow`/`unborrow` pair (`mesh.py`) snapshots `was_resident`,\n542\tloads an on-demand model on a Tiiny, serves, then restores — during that window\n543\tthe resident specialist is gone; and any route whose `policy` entry points at a\n544\tTiiny will trigger it. Read `policy` before a long run: an `image_gen` primary\n545\tof `tiiny-octo`, an `embedding` secondary of `tiiny-ant/...`, or a `chat`\n546\tsecondary on the other box are each an eviction waiting for one request.\n547\t\n548\t- **`ensure` replaces it.** Before delegating, make the model resident:\n549\t\n550\t```bash\n551\tpython3 scripts/tiiny_ctl.py ensure --model \"Qwen/Qwen3.6-35B-A3B\"\n552\t```\n553\t\n554\tIt is idempotent, it checks the cross-agent lock, it evicts explicitly when a\n555\tmodel does not fit, and it exits non-zero when a model did not land. The two\n556\tscan jobs run it before they dispatch a child — if `ensure` fails, they do not\n557\tdelegate, because a missing model means a 404 and a silent fallback to the\n558\tpaid provider.\n559\t\n560\t**The router calls `ensure` automatically.** When `ENSURE_ON_ROUTE` is `True`\n561\t(the default), `route_task` calls `ctl.ensure_model(model, quiet=True)` after\n562\tchoosing the model and before returning the route. This makes the device ready\n563\tfor the child without an extra manual step. The call is idempotent — when the\n564\tmodel is already running, it costs one HTTP round-trip. When it is not, the\n565\tcontroller loads it and verifies the load before returning. Exceptions are\n566\tsilently caught so a load failure cannot break routing — the device returns 404\n567\tand Hermes falls back to the paid provider as expected.\n568\t\n569\tTo disable, set the environment variable ``TIINY_ENSURE_ON_ROUTE=false`` or\n570\tedit ``ENSURE_ON_ROUTE`` in the plugin.\n571\t\n572\t**One list, one file.** `REQUIRED_MODELS` in `scripts/tiiny_ctl.py` is the\n573\tonly copy. Every other location — `~/.hermes/scripts/`, both skill tiers,\n574\t`~/.hermes/shared-skills/` — is a **symlink** into the canonical file in the\n575\tvault, so a change cannot fork. Only the publish tree holds a second real\n576\tcopy, because it ships to GitHub and git cannot publish a link; the deploy\n577\tstep regenerates it. The mechanics — why each skill root still needs its own\n578\tdirectory, why a symlink in a git tree shows as a typechange, and how to\n579\tconvert an existing set of copies — are in\n580\t[references/single-source-deployment.md](references/single-source-deployment.md).\n581\t\n582\t**Trimming is a functional decision, not just an NPU decision.** Hermes's own\n583\t`stt` and `tts` providers used to point at the appliance, and TTS has no\n584\ton-demand load path — a TTS model that is not resident makes `text_to_speech`\n585\tfail. That constraint is gone: speech now lives on the Mac's OffGrid gateway,\n586\twhich serves it from its own process at no NPU cost. See\n587\t`mlops/offgrid-local-inference`. The appliance's speech models were unloaded\n588\ton 2026-11-10 and are no longer in the required set.\n589\t\n590\t- **Two boxes need a per-box capability map, not one table.** The two boxes carry\n591\t  the SAME store — verified 2026-11-13: identical 93 model ids, and identical\n592\t  `npu_usage` / `context_window` / `type` / `capabilities` / `params` for every\n593\t  one of them. They differ only in what is on disk and what is resident, so\n594\t  \"which model do I ask for\" is a question about download state, never about\n595\t  configuration. `references/two-box-capability-matrix.md` has the endpoint map,\n596\t  the NPU-budget findings, and the classification rules; `scripts/tiiny_two_box_matrix.py`\n597\t  regenerates and self-verifies the whole matrix read-only.\n598\t- **The NPU budget is 48 GiB and only one endpoint says so.**\n599\t  `GET /api/v1/npu/status` -> `devices[0].mem_total_mb` = 49024 (LQ50-48GB).\n600\t  The catalogue's `npu_usage` is percent of that pool (resident declares 47,\n601\t  device measures 46.30%), so a co-resident set must sum to <= 100.\n602\t  `GET /api/v1/sys/status` -> `npus[].memory_total_mb` is NOT the budget — it\n603\t  moves between samples and never equals 49024. Every other `/api/v1/npu/*`\n604\t  path 404s.\n605\t- **The device has no code flag, and a naive \"does the desc contain 'coding'\"\n606\t  misfires.** The word appears as a purpose (\"is a coding-specialized model\"),\n607\t  as a training-data footnote (gpt-oss-20b/120b) and as a benchmark aside\n608\t  (Qwen3.5-9B). Only the first is a capability. See\n609\t  `references/two-box-capability-matrix.md` for the rule and the models it\n610\t  separates.\n611\t\n612\t`references/required-set-drift.md` keeps the audit recipe for \"who changed the\n613\tresident set, and when\" — still worth reading, because the mechanism that\n614\tcaused those drifts (one policy, many copies) is exactly what the symlinks\n615\tnow make impossible.\n616\t\n617\t## Scheduled work on the device\n618\t\n619\tDelegated children are not the only thing that can run on the box — a\n620\tscheduled job can, and a scheduled job is the better host for work that has\n621\tto happen unattended every day. The two differ in one way that matters:\n622\t**a cron job's provider is pinned per job**, so a box that is down fails\n623\tthe job instead of quietly falling through to a paid model.\n624\t\n625\t### Pin a job to the box\n626\t\n627\t```bash\n628\thermes cron edit <job_id> --model \"Qwen/Qwen3.6-35B-A3B\" --provider custom:tiiny --pin\n629\t```\n630\t\n631\tPrecedence is **per-job pin > `cron.model_provider` > the persisted global\n632\tconfig**, and the model follows the same order (`cron.model` > the main\n633\tagent model). Set the defaults once and a job created later starts on the\n634\tbox without anyone pinning it:\n635\t\n636\t```bash\n637\thermes config set cron.model \"Qwen/Qwen3.6-35B-A3B\"\n638\thermes config set cron.model_provider custom:tiiny\n639\t```\n640\t\n641\t### The pin is what makes the saving real\n642\t\n643\tA job that carries its own `model`, `provider` or `base_url` gets **no\n644\tfallback chain at all** — it does not inherit `fallback_providers`, so a box\n645\tthat is down produces a **failed job with a visible error** and a zero bill.\n646\tAn unpinned job inherits the chain and can quietly spend. Pin the job, and\n647\tthe failure is legible instead of expensive.\n648\t\n649\t### Do not route scheduled work through the mesh\n650\t\n651\tThe mesh is a router, and a router's **secondary is a paid provider**. A\n652\tchat policy whose secondary is `openrouter/...` falls through to it the\n653\tmoment the box misses — so the saving disappears exactly when the box is\n654\tbusy. **Point scheduled work at a device provider block directly.** It is\n655\talso materially faster: a live call measured ~2.4 s direct against ~34 s\n656\tthrough the mesh.\n657\t\n658\t### Look for the provider block before you write one\n659\t\n660\t`providers:` usually already carries a block per box — `tiiny`, `tiiny2`,\n661\t`tiinymesh` — each complete with its own `key_env` and model list. Grep\n662\tthe config before writing a block: one that already exists needs no edit\n663\tand no restart, and a **new** block is not live until the client restarts,\n664\tbecause provider blocks are read at startup.\n665\t\n666\t### Contention is decided by agent windows, not by the clock\n667\t\n668\tA scheduled job's **script phase does no model work** — the script collects\n669\tdata and the agent interprets it. Only the agent phase occupies the device.\n670\tSo two jobs whose schedules are minutes apart may not contend at all, and\n671\ttwo jobs hours apart certainly can. Place jobs by when each one's **agent**\n672\tphase runs, and give the longest agent phase a box to itself: two boxes are\n673\ttwo free streams.\n674\t\n675\t### The cron inactivity watchdog\n676\t\n677\t`HERMES_CRON_TIMEOUT` is the limit on how long a job may be **silent**\n678\tbefore the scheduler declares it dead. It is **not** a cap on total\n679\truntime. The default is 600 s, sized for a provider that answers in\n680\tseconds; a local model on a long context can be quiet for a minute or two\n681\tbetween turns. Raise it when jobs start dying at the watchdog rather than\n682\tat the model. Both the inactivity monitor and the cwd-lock bound derive\n683\tfrom this one value, so they cannot drift apart.\n684\t\n685\t`~/.hermes/.env` is a protected credential file with no CLI of its own —\n686\thand the human a script for it (see the pitfall below).\n687\t\n688\t## Pitfalls\n689\t\n690\t- **A load is a swap.** Loading a model evicts the resident one; the evicted\n691\t  model's endpoint then returns `404`, which means *not resident*, not a bad\n692\t  model id. Restore the required set when the work is done.\n693\t- **The device decides what to evict — unless you decide first.** Left alone\n694\t  it kept the 47% generalist and evicted the 27% model the batch had just\n695\t  asked for. The controller evicts explicitly, keeping the cheapest models\n696\t  that fit. Never let a bare `tiiny load` be the only thing that touches the\n697\t  device during a batch.\n698\t- **A cold device bills you, and says nothing.** The appliance unloads on\n699\t  reboot. There is no poller putting models back any more, so the *parent*\n700\t  must run `ensure` before it delegates. Skip that and the child gets a 404\n701\t  which Hermes classifies as terminal and answers from the paid provider.\n702\t  Nothing on a timer unloads a model either — an eviction comes from another\n703\t  load, which in practice means a mesh `policy` route pointing at the wrong\n704\t  box. Read `policy` before a long run, not the clock.\n705\t\n706\t- **A logged-out CLI session looks exactly like a cold device, and it is\n707\t  the more dangerous of the two.** `tiiny load` opens a device-admin session.\n708\t  With no session it fails with \"Not logged in. Use 'tiiny login' to sign\n709\t  in\", nothing becomes resident, and `ensure` has to notice. The load verdict\n710\t  therefore never claims a model was loaded when it was not: it prints\n711\t  `NOT RESIDENT` on its own, never `loaded: X` next to `NOT RESIDENT: X`.\n712\t  A batch that reads only the exit code and skips the log still catches it —\n713\t  the exit code is non-zero. Check the session before blaming the NPU.\n714\t\n715\t- **The catalogue is the device's, not the table's.** Read `GET\n716\t  /api/v1/models/online_models` and use each entry's `npu_usage` and\n717\t  `context_window`. When two boxes are in play, compare their catalogues\n718\t  against each other rather than assuming one table fits both: measured\n719\t  2026-11-12, the Pocket and the Octo reported identical model sets and\n720\t  identical NPU and context figures for every tier model, while stocking\n721\t  them differently (16 models on one, 42 on the other). That is what makes\n722\t  one box the pinned vault and the other the flex box.\n723\t\n724\t- **Two boxes need two locks, two holds and two plans — and one endpoint\n725\t  each.** The shared state (lock, hold, plan, catalogue snapshot) is\n726\t  device-scoped through `TIINY_LOCK_FILE`, `TIINY_HOLD_FILE`,\n727\t  `TIINY_PLAN_FILE` and `TIINY_SNAPSHOT_FILE`, and the endpoints through\n728\t  `TIINY_HOST` and `TIINY_BASE_URL`. The defaults are the single-device\n729\t  paths, so nothing moves unless a caller asks. The alias\n730\t  `openai.api.tiiny` resolves to the desktop app's forwarder and serves ONE\n731\t  device at a time — a second box has to be addressed by its own IP.\n732\t\n733\t- **The controller drives ONE box; the mesh lifecycle route drives both.**\n734\t  `tiiny_ctl.py` reads the bearer from the literal name `TIINY_API_KEY` only\n735\t  (there is no `TIINY_API_KEY_2` fallback), one `MANAGEMENT_BASE` from\n736\t  `TIINY_HOST`, and shells its load/unload out to `tiiny load`, whose session\n737\t  is bound to a single device. To reach the second box with it you must export\n738\t  that box's key **as** `TIINY_API_KEY` in that shell, plus its `TIINY_HOST`\n739\t  and its own lock/hold/plan/snapshot files. For both boxes at once, use\n740\t  `POST /mesh/lifecycle` `{action, model, target}` on the mesh gateway, or the\n741\t  box's own `POST /api/v1/models/<id>/start|stop` with its bearer — both run\n742\t  concurrently, no CLI session involved. Recipe and the per-box error\n743\t  asymmetry: [references/two-box-capability-matrix.md](references/two-box-capability-matrix.md#driving-both-boxes-at-once).\n744\t\n745\t- **Downloading a model is a separate path from loading one.** `POST\n746\t  /api/v1/models/<id>/download` pulls from the store onto the device's disk,\n747\t  authorised with the box's bearer and needing no CLI session; `tiiny download\n748\t  <model_id> --device-address <ip>` does the same thing through the CLI, where\n749\t  it deadlocks after the transfer and always exits non-zero. A download\n750\t  costs no NPU — the model sits on disk until something loads it, which is\n751\t  where the swap budget applies. `tiiny ls -r` is the store catalogue,\n752\t  `tiiny ls -d` is what is on disk, and the difference is the download list.\n753\t  `tiiny import` covers models the store does not carry.\n754\t\n755\t- **The CLI's exit code, and its output after a transfer, are not the\n756\t  truth.** A completed download dies with `fatal error: all goroutines are\n757\t  asleep - deadlock!` — the command's own progress reporter parks on a\n758\t  channel that never signals, and the process exits non-zero every time.\n759\t  The \"already downloaded\" path exits non-zero with a perfectly normal\n760\t  sentence. Verify every download against the device's own catalogue entry\n761\t  — `status`, `progress`, and the byte count — not against the CLI's exit\n762\t  code. Same rule as the load verdict: the device decides.\n763\t\n764\t- **`tiiny load` and `tiiny unload` need a real terminal.** In a\n765\t  non-interactive shell they fail with \"could not open a new TTY\" and\n766\t  nothing becomes resident — a silent no-op that looks like a refusal to\n767\t  load. Run them inside a pseudo-terminal (`script -q /dev/null tiiny load\n768\t  ...`) when there is no TTY. The appliance exposes no SSH shell to borrow\n769\t  one from — ports 22 and 2222 are closed on both boxes, and the device's\n770\t  36-route API has no shell, exec or console route — and `--json` does not\n771\t  skip the prompt. Any controlling terminal works: the `script` wrapper, or\n772\t  a runner that allocates one.\n773\t\n774\t- **The control CLI holds ONE session, bound to ONE device.** Signing in\n775\t  for a second box signs the first one *out* — the stored session is a\n776\t  single opaque token, not a map of devices. The CLI can therefore manage\n777\t  one box at a time, and moving the session between boxes is a human step\n778\t  (a sign-in with a code). Two rules follow: every control call has to\n779\t  name its device — `tiiny_ctl.py` resolves `TIINY_DEVICE_ADDRESS`, then\n780\t  `TIINY_HOST`, then the CLI config, and passes `--device-address` to\n781\t  every load and unload — and a controller aimed at a box that does not\n782\t  hold the session must say so, rather than failing as though the model\n783\t  were the problem. The API credentials are unaffected: each box's bearer\n784\t  is independent, and both boxes serve inference at the same time.\n785\t\n786\t- **A script that edits a credentials file must write atomically.** A\n787\t  mistyped `open(path, \"w\", encoding=...)` truncated the file before it\n788\t  raised. Write through a temp file and `os.replace` it into place, and\n789\t  prove the change against a copy — the real config is the one file that\n790\t  cannot be rebuilt from a backup of itself.\n791\t\n792\t- **The client config is off-limits to the agent; hand the human a script.**\n793\t  `~/.hermes/config.yaml` is guarded by design, and the right response is\n794\t  not to route around the guard: write the change as a script the human\n795\t  runs — a timestamped backup, one line edited, a temp file and `os.replace`\n796\t  into place, a printed revert command, and a proof run against a COPY of\n797\t  the config first. The human restarts the client afterwards anyway, and a\n798\t  provider block is config they need to see. **A provider block is read at\n799\t  startup, so a newly added provider is not live until the client\n800\t  restarts** — say that when the change is handed over, because a provider\n801\t  that appears configured but answers nothing looks like a routing fault.\n802\t  `hermes config set KEY VAL` covers `config.yaml`; `~/.hermes/.env` has no\n803\t  CLI of its own, so a credential-file change is always the hand-run\n804\t  script.\n805\t- **A revert must remove what you added, not rewind the file.** The newest\n806\t  backup of a file you have already edited still contains your own change,\n807\t  so restoring it does not revert anything — it just restores your last\n808\t  state. Revert by removing the one key you added, leaving every other\n809\t  byte alone, and keep the backup for the case where the file itself is\n810\t  wrong.\n811\t\n812\t- **Different form factors mean different NPU figures.** A Pocket and an\n813\t  Octo are not the same silicon. The tier table's shares were measured on\n814\t  one of them; re-measure the other (`catalog --refresh` pointed at it)\n815\t  before routing anything to it.\n816\t\n817\t- **Do not hard-code a device's error strings.** A signature-based LAN scan\n818\t  that matched on `{\"error\":\"auth_failed\"}` found nothing after the device's\n819\t  OS update renamed it to `{\"error\":\"unauthorized\",\"reason\":\"missing_token\"}`.\n820\t  Match on the route and the status code, and read the body for a human —\n821\t  never a pinned string.\n822\t\n823\t- **A device's display name is not its identity.** A Tiiny was \"Tiiny\n824\t  Pocket\" until it was renamed \"TinyAnt\" in the desktop app, and the CLI\n825\t  scan, the device's own `device_name` and the account view each showed a\n826\t  different string. Key every record, lock, plan and provider on the\n827\t  **device ID** (`TNYM…`) and treat the name as a label.\n828\t\n829\t- **Each box has its own device-admin bearer, and the alias fronts only\n830\t  one of them.** `tiiny auth key --device-address <ip>` issues a credential\n831\t  for a specific box; the account-level key opens one box's store and\n832\t  inference while the other answers \"Not authenticated\" on the same\n833\t  routes. The alias `openai.api.tiiny` is a local nginx forwarder that\n834\t  holds the credentials and serves the app's currently selected box — one\n835\t  at a time. That is not a hardware limit: the boxes are separate hosts\n836\t  with separate NPUs, and one answers while the other refuses. Two-box\n837\t  parallelism needs two bearers and two direct addresses.\n838\t\n839\t- **A device stays closed until the desktop TiinyOS app has an\n840\t  authenticated session for it.** The gate is the app's session, not the\n841\t  bearer: with no app session the CLI and the API both refuse, and the\n842\t  routes answer 401 / \"Not authenticated\" even with a valid key. One box\n843\t  can be open while its sibling is closed. The CLI session is separate\n844\t  again — `tiiny login` signs the CLI in, and it is what `tiiny load` and\n845\t  `tiiny auth key` need.\n846\t\n847\t- **The CLI session is bound to the device it was signed in for.** Point it\n848\t  at another box and it reports \"Not logged in\" even though the account\n849\t  session is live — `tiiny auth key --device-address <ip>` issues a\n850\t  credential for that box only to a session bound to that box. So each box\n851\t  needs its own `tiiny login`, and each has its own bearer: two valid keys\n852\t  existed for one box while neither opened the other. The alias rejects a\n853\t  client-supplied bearer outright (401 even with a valid device key) — it\n854\t  is app-internal, not an endpoint an agent can use.\n855\t\n856\t- **The alias is the app's forwarder, so address the box.** `openai.api.tiiny`\n857\t  resolves to the desktop app's forwarder on 127.0.0.1, and that forwarder\n858\t  follows the app's session and its currently selected device — it answers\n859\t  for one box at a time and 401s whenever the session does not cover it. A\n860\t  device is addressed by its own IP. The cost is that an IP needs a DHCP\n861\t  reservation, because a provider pinned to a wandering address fails\n862\t  silently as a fallback to the paid provider.\n863\t\n864\t- **Serving and loading are different paths.** A model that is already\n865\t  running answers immediately — no CLI session needed. The CLI session is\n866\t  needed only by the CLI, and the device's HTTP API does load, unload and\n867\t  download without one. So a box with a warm model is usable while the CLI\n868\t  is logged out, and a box that is *not* the signed-in one can still be\n869\t  loaded, unloaded and stocked.\n870\t\n871\t- **The device's published route map is not proof of what it will accept.**\n872\t  `GET /openapi.json` lists 36 paths and none of them is model lifecycle —\n873\t  and the device still answers `POST /api/v1/models/<id>/download`,\n874\t  `/start` and `/stop`. **A spec describes the system; the system decides\n875\t  what it accepts.** Never write \"the device has no route for X\" from a\n876\t  route map — POST it and read the status code. Probing an undocumented\n877\t  route costs one request; publishing the absence costs a correction, and a\n878\t  published correction stays in the commit history permanently.\n879\t\n880\t- **Three things decide which box a CLI command reaches: the flag, the\n881\t  config, and the token — and they do not agree by default.**\n882\t  `--device-address <ip>` reaches the box you named even when the config\n883\t  points elsewhere; with no flag the CLI follows the config's\n884\t  `deviceAddress`; and the stored token decides whether you are authorised\n885\t  for whichever box is selected. So the flag **selects** the target and the\n886\t  token **grants** access, and a session for one box will not open the\n887\t  other however you address it. Pass the address on every control call, and\n888\t  read a \"Not logged in\" answer as \"this token is for another box\", not as\n889\t  \"the device is down\".\n890\t\n891\t- **The store entry is the download truth, not a storage endpoint.**\n892\t  `GET /api/v1/models/online_models` carries each model's `status`\n893\t  (`running` / `downloaded` / `downloading` / `not_downloaded`),\n894\t  `progress` and `npu_usage`. `/api/v1/sys/storage/llm` reported\n895\t  `total_size: 0` while eight models were downloaded and one was running —\n896\t  it is not a model-presence measure, and reading it as one invents a\n897\t  missing-models finding.\n898\t\n899\t- **A revoked bearer fails as a 401, and a stale one fails quietly\n900\t  enough.** When it was revoked, every call returned 401 `auth_failed`\n901\t  while the controller still reported \"key: found\" — found in the\n902\t  environment, not valid at the device. Check the device's answer, not the\n903\t  key's presence. The bearer is **device-scoped**: each box issues its own\n904\t  from `tiiny auth key --device-address <ip>`, and one box's bearer answers\n905\t  401 on the sibling box's routes.\n906\t- **Two heavy children deadlock.** Not an error — a hang, with the device\n907\t  still reporting healthy. One heavy child at a time.\n908\t- **Dead fallbacks crash the run.** When a local model times out or stalls,\n909\t  Hermes aborts the connection and silently falls back to\n910\t  `delegation.fallback_providers`. If that fallback provider is exhausted\n911\t  (e.g., MiniMax returning HTTP 429), the run fails immediately. Ensure\n912\t  fallback points to an active provider with working credits (e.g., OpenRouter).\n913\t  The `/mesh/fallback` route on TiinyMesh flips both the mesh chat secondary and\n914\t  Hermes's `delegation.fallback_providers` in lockstep between OpenRouter and MiniMax.\n915\t- **OpenRouter authentication & CA certificates on macOS.** Standard\n916\t  `urllib.request` on macOS Python fails with `CERTIFICATE_VERIFY_FAILED`\n917\t  against OpenRouter unless the system CA store / `certifi` bundle is loaded\n918\t  (as in `mesh._ssl_context()`). OpenRouter's `/api/v1/credits` strictly\n919\t  requires a management key (`OPENROUTER_MANAGEMENT_KEY`) and returns 403 on\n920\t  inference keys; use `GET /api/v1/key` to check key spend and caps when only\n921\t  an inference key is configured. Active keys may live in `~/.hermes/auth.json`\n922\t  under `credential_pool` even when `~/.hermes/.env` holds a stale key.\n923\t- **Large context causes NPU PCIe HDMA timeouts on 30B models.**\n924\t  `Qwen/Qwen3-Coder-30B-A3B-Instruct-Turbo` has a 65K context ceiling, but on\n925\t  hardware when prompt context approaches ~44K+ tokens, the device driver\n926\t  times out (`pcie_hdma_timeout`) and hangs without closing the socket. After\n927\t  180 seconds with no tokens, Hermes's stale-call watchdog aborts the connection\n928\t  and silently falls back to `fallback_providers` (e.g., MiniMax). Keep prompts\n929\t  under 30K tokens on 30B models, or use a 256K model (`Qwen3.6-35B-A3B` or\n930\t  `Qwen3.8-27B`) for large context.\n931\t- **Goal keywords override model intent in delegation.** The router infers\n932\t  tiers from goal text (\"execute\", \"code\", \"run\" -> `tier: code`). A goal\n933\t  stating \"execute the sprint plan\" routes to the 65K Coder model instead of\n934\t  the 256K generalist or planner. Pass `tier=\"general\"` or `tier=\"browser\"`\n935\t  explicitly when the task needs long context over coding specialization.\n936\t- **Concurrent loads trigger PCIe HDMA timeouts.** Sending overlapping\n937\t  model-load or lifecycle commands can cause the device driver to hit\n938\t  `pcie_hdma_timeout` (\"NPU PCIe HDMA transfer timed out\"), emptying\n939\t  `devices: []` and evicting all models (`running: []`). Always serialize\n940\t  model loads sequentially per box.\n941\t- **`route_check.py` does not take a `--model` flag.** It infers the tier from\n942\t  goal text or accepts `--tier <name>` or `--all`. Passing `--model` is an\n943\t  unrecognized argument error. Preflight with `--tier` or the goal string.\n944\t- **`tiiny ls` shows size, not residency.** Its NPU column is how much NPU a\n945\t  model takes, not whether it is loaded. `tiiny_ctl.py status` is the\n946\t  residency truth.\n947\t- **A 404 is not a typo.** Before concluding a model id is wrong, check\n948\t  residency. A model can be in the Store and not loaded.\n949\t- **A FORCED tier overrides residency, and that is how work silently moves\n950\t  to the paid provider.** The router's inference respects what is loaded;\n951\t  `delegate_task(tier=...)` does not — it selects the tier's first choice\n952\t  and sends it. If that model is not resident the device answers 404, Hermes\n953\t  treats 404 as terminal, and the child falls back to the paid provider\n954\t  *invisibly*, with a good answer and a bill. Measured 2026-11-13: forcing\n955\t  `tier=\"code\"` selected `Qwen/Qwen3-Coder-30B-A3B-Instruct-Turbo`\n956\t  (\"NOT loaded\") where inference alone had selected the resident\n957\t  `Qwen/Qwen3.6-35B-A3B`. **Run `route_check.py` before every dispatch and\n958\t  only force a tier it reports as loaded.** The tier is a hint; residency\n959\t  is the constraint.\n960\t- **Context window is a ceiling, not a suggestion.** A child that silently\n961\t  truncates its context is usually routed to a model whose window is too\n962\t  small for the task, not to a broken task.\n963\t- **A level inside `chat_template_kwargs` is not the same knob as a level at\n964\t  the top level of the request.** The chat-template one is read by some model\n965\t  families and silently ignored by others — a model whose chat template has\n966\t  no boolean switch reads the level only at the top level of the request.\n967\t  Putting it in the wrong place looks configured and changes nothing.\n968\t- **`enable_thinking: false` is mandatory on every Tiiny route.** It is the\n969\t  provider's `extra_body` and it survives the route's own overrides only\n970\t  where the route does not set its own thinking state. Without it, every\n971\t  child reasons by default, on every task, including the mechanical ones.\n972\t- **A reasoning model with a small `max_tokens` returns an empty response**\n973\t  with the budget spent in the trace, and the status code is 200. The router\n974\t  applies an output floor for this reason; if you set `max_tokens` yourself,\n975\t  keep it above the floor.\n976\t- **The device's `toggleable` flag has been wrong.** A model flagged as\n977\t  having no on/off switch honours it anyway. Send the boolean whenever it can\n978\t  be sent, and let the label report what actually took effect.\n979\t- **A stale hold makes `status` lie.** With no poller to disable, a leftover\n980\t  hold no longer breaks the device — it just makes `status` report a resident\n981\t  set that is no longer true. `release` clears it and verifies.\n982\t- **Trimming the device is not trimming the policy.** `release` restores\n983\t  `REQUIRED_MODELS`, so unloading a model without editing that list means it\n984\t  returns at the next release and the change looks like it never applied.\n985\t  Edit the list first, then unload. See \"The required set\" above.\n986\t- **One thinking model hides it in its prose.** `trohrbaugh/Qwen3.8-27B-heretic-ara-Turbo` reports `thinking.supported:true` (switch + levels `low/medium/xhigh`, 56% NPU) but its `desc` never mentions \"thinking\" or \"reasoning\" — read `thinking` from `GET /api/v1/models/online_models` at route time, not from the description, or you will burn a trace you never asked for. On 2026-11-10 this was the only one of 22 thinking models with hidden prose.\n987\t- **Absolute paths and vault-local prefixes are publish-blockers.** The\n988\t  publisher's scanner forbids absolute home paths and any vault-local\n989\t  prefix — a catalogue footer that spells out a home directory, the vault\n990\t  root, or the publish tree is a `PUBLISH-SAFETY` finding and blocks the\n991\t  push. Write review locations as a plain relative name and scrub before\n992\t  pushing; JSON must also scan `CLEAN` before it ships as\n993\t  `data/catalogue.json`. This bullet once named the forbidden patterns\n994\t  literally, which made the skill's own file fail the scan it describes.\n995\t- **A dangling link inside a skill is usually drift, not a missing file.**\n996\t  Two broken links in one deployed copy of this skill pointed at reference\n997\t  documents the upstream source had since replaced or renamed — that copy\n998\t  was an older revision nobody had re-synced. Patching the link text would\n999\t  have left it diverging from upstream in every other way. **Sync the whole\n1000\t  skill instead, then fix what the sync breaks.** Two consequences worth\n1001\t  knowing before you start: the upstream copy's new files may carry a device\n1002\t  serial or a private LAN address that the publish scanner refuses, so the\n1003\t  sync needs a **build-time redaction transform** in the publisher (redact\n1004\t  the published bytes, never the source — the running controller needs the\n1005\t  real addresses); and a deployed copy may hold files upstream does not\n1006\t  have, so a wholesale copy over it deletes working code. Diff before\n1007\t  overwriting, and check the hash of every file you did not intend to change.\n1008\t\n1009\t- **The vault copy is the DEPLOY SOURCE — write there first.** The routing\n1010\t  layer lives in several places, and `sync-tiiny-router-skill.sh` copies\n1011\t  **from** `skills/tiiny-model-routing/` **to** three live skill roots and\n1012\t  the plugin directory, because the Hermes install copies are overwritten on\n1013\t  every Hermes update and the vault copy is the one that survives. A hand\n1014\t  edit to a live copy is therefore a change that **the next deploy erases**.\n1015\t  When you add a lesson to this skill, add it to the vault copy first and\n1016\t  let the deploy carry it out. Editing the profile copy alone leaves the two\n1017\t  divergent in both directions at once: the live copy has the change the\n1018\t  vault does not, and the vault has everything else the live copy does not.\n1019\t\n1020\t- **The deploy script's file filter omits `.pytest_cache`.** It excludes\n1021\t  `__pycache__` and `*.pyc`, so a test run that leaves a `.pytest_cache/`\n1022\t  directory in `scripts/` gets that directory **copied into every live\n1023\t  skill root** on the next deploy. Do not run the deploy script with a\n1024\t  dirty `.pytest_cache` present until the filter is widened.\n1025\t- **This skill never downloads a model.** It chooses and loads from what is\n1026\t  already on the device.\n1027\t\n1028\t## Delegating coding work\n1029\t\n1030\tThe discipline for making the appliance do the writing and the manager\n1031\tagent do the judging. The full method is in\n1032\t[references/coding-delegation.md](references/coding-delegation.md).\n1033\t\n1034\t### Two rules from the author that decide whether you delegate at all\n1035\t\n1036\t**A multi-part job delegates; it does not get done inline.** When a task has\n1037\tseveral parts — a sweep, a rewrite, a build, a set of independent edits — hand\n1038\tthe mechanical parts to a sub-agent on the appliance rather than spending the\n1039\tpaid model's tokens on them. The appliance has no rate limit and no bill, so a\n1040\tlarge job is free there and expensive here. **The manager keeps the diagnosis,\n1041\tthe review and the final write** — and \"the final write\" means the edits the\n1042\tsub-agent's own verification could not make trustworthy.\n1043\t\n1044\t**When you are curious about something, ask the author — do not investigate it.**\n1045\tCuriosity is a reason to ask a question, not a reason to spend a search. The\n1046\tfailure mode is specific: a manager that cannot verify a claim from the\n1047\toutside spends many tool calls proving a thing it was already told, and the\n1048\tuser watches the token bill grow for a result they could have stated. If the author\n1049\tsays he posted it, that **is** the evidence — record it and move on. If you\n1050\tneed to know *why* he did something, ask; the answer costs one message and\n1051\tteaches the rule you did not have.\n1052\t\n1053\tThe test for whether a lookup is worth its cost: **will the answer change what\n1054\tI write?** If not, it is curiosity — ask instead of digging.\n1055\t\n1056\t- **The manager does not write the code.** Reaching for `patch` or\n1057\t  `write_file` on the target file is the expensive model doing the worker's\n1058\t  job, which is the whole thing this discipline exists to prevent. The\n1059\t  manager writes the order, reads the diff, runs the acceptance and\n1060\t  decides. Exception: a one-edit correction where a delegation round trip\n1061\t  would cost more than the fix — make it, and say so plainly.\n1062\t- **Make the box match the router, not the router match the box.** To use a\n1063\t  specialist, put it on the box `TIINY_BASE_URL` already probes. Do not\n1064\t  repoint the endpoint or wait for a restart — `TIINY_BASE_URL` is read at\n1065\t  import time, so it only changes in a new process, and a downloaded model\n1066\t  costs nothing to load where the router is already looking.\n1067\t- **Preflight every dispatch.** `route_check.py --thinking off \"<goal>\"` and\n1068\t  read the residency line. If it says NOT loaded, do not force that tier.\n1069\t- **Order format.** One job, one file, exact absolute paths, exact anchors\n1070\t  (the function, the literal string), a runnable acceptance command, and\n1071\t  no `<...>` placeholders — the delegation backend refuses those outright.\n1072\t  A 30B appliance needs a tighter brief than a frontier model does.\n1073\t- **A child's summary is a self-report.** Run the acceptance command\n1074\t  yourself. Correct with a diff and the failure output as evidence, never\n1075\t  a re-brief. **Make the acceptance command resolve what the child\n1076\t  changed**, not merely assert that a string is present — a path that\n1077\t  is present but does not resolve from its file's directory is a broken\n1078\t  path wearing a green check. And a rewrite that alters a path *mentioned\n1079\t  inside prose* has changed what the sentence says; read the changed\n1080\t  lines in context before accepting the batch.\n1081\t- **The route label is the invoice.** `tiiny-router` means the box served\n1082\t  it; anything else means the paid provider did. Watch it every wave.\n1083\t- **One heavy child per box.** Two boxes are two free streams.\n1084\t- **Release after every wave.** Stop the specialist, restore the required\n1085\t  set.\n1086\t\n1087\tThe work-order format and the fallback alarm live in\n1088\t[references/coding-delegation.md](references/coding-delegation.md);\n1089\t[templates/work-order.md](templates/work-order.md) is a starter to copy\n1090\tfor each order.\n1091\t\n1092\t## Verification\n1093\t\n1094\t\n1095\t\n1096\t```bash\n1097\tpython3 scripts/tiiny_ctl.py status                  # device, residency, lock, hold, plan\n1098\tpython3 scripts/tiiny_ctl.py plan --goal \"your goal\" # tier, model, NPU fit, waves\n1099\tpython3 scripts/route_check.py --all                 # every tier and its budget\n1100\tpython3 scripts/route_check.py --thinking off \"goal\" # tier, model, and the reasoning state\n1101\tpython3 scripts/tiiny_ctl.py catalog --refresh       # what moved in the Model Store\n1102\t```\n1103\t\n1104\tA batch is correct when the plan's model matches the shape of the work, every\n1105\twave fits inside 100% NPU, every wave's models were resident when the child\n1106\tran, the child's route label says what you expected, the child's artifact\n1107\tsurvives its own claim, and the device was released at the end.\n1108\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"95803e3d-040d-4401-b98c-4ca7b716b706","uuid":"4ba16828-d02f-48ad-85af-6aab3c244b72","timestamp":"2026-11-21T05:58:38.764Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/nick/code/tiiny_router_hermes.hermes-plugin-package-12-docs-for-package-install/tiiny-router/SKILL.md","content":"---\nname: tiiny-model-routing\ndescription: \"Run delegated sub-agents on the Tiiny device. Chooses the model that fits each subtask, loads it, locks the device against other agents, supervises the child, verifies its work, and releases the device.\"\nversion: 2.1.0\nauthor: Gary Garcia (garciacionet-alt), Hermes Agent\nlicense: MIT\nplatforms: [linux, macos]\nmetadata:\n  hermes:\n    tags: [tiiny, delegation, model-routing, local-inference, agents, locking, lifecycle, thinking]\n---\n\n# Tiiny Model Routing\n\nLet a manager agent use the Tiiny appliance as the host for its sub-agents:\nchoose the model that fits each subtask, load it, keep other agents off the\ndevice while it works, supervise the child, check what it produced, and hand\nthe device back.\n\nThe shape of it: **a frontier model on a paid plan supervises, and the device\ndoes the work.** The paid model stays in the main loop — that is where\njudgement and conversation belong — and everything token-heavy goes down to\nthe box on the LAN. The paid subscription stops paying for volume, and the\ndiversity of the Model Store becomes available to every batch.\n\n## When to Use\n\n- A delegation batch mixes different kinds of work — a research sweep, a\n  filesystem index, a code edit, a browser run, a summary.\n- The subtasks are big enough that the paid provider's cost and rate limits\n  matter.\n- Another agent in the AMC might want the device at the same time, and the\n  two need to be able to see that it is spoken for.\n- You need an image and want the right image model rather than the default.\n\nDon't use for: a single-model deployment where every subtask is the same kind\nof work (set `delegation.model` and stop — but still `ensure` that model is\nresident first, because nothing else will), or non-Tiiny providers (the\nmechanism is provider-agnostic, but the tier table is Tiiny-specific).\n\n**The lightweight path skips most of this skill.** When the batch needs one\nknown model and no wave planning, the whole job is:\n\n```bash\npython3 scripts/tiiny_ctl.py ensure --model \"Qwen/Qwen3.6-35B-A3B\"\n```\n\nthen delegate. `ensure` is idempotent and lock-aware, and it is what the\nscheduled scan jobs run as their first step. Steps 2-5 and 9 below are for the\ncase where the models are not yet known, or the batch spans several.\n\n## Prerequisites\n\n- A Tiiny device reachable on the LAN, with its bearer available as\n  `TIINY_API_KEY` (Hermes keeps it in `~/.hermes/.env`; the controller finds\n  it there, in the environment, or in the vault's credentials file).\n- The `tiiny` CLI at `~/.local/tiiny/bin/tiiny` for the load and unload\n  commands.\n- **A logged-in CLI session.** The CLI is one path to the model lifecycle; the device's HTTP API is another.\n  The device publishes a route map at `GET /openapi.json`, and it does **not**\n  list the model lifecycle routes — but the routes work. `POST\n  /api/v1/models/<id>/download` and `/start` and `/stop` are all\n  live on the device, authorised with the box's bearer, and they need **no session**.\n  `tiiny login` is interactive (email + code) and is a human step.\n- The routing backend plugin installed (see [references/backend.md](references/backend.md)), and\n  `delegation.backend: tiiny-router` set in the active profile's config.\n  This skill ships inside the plugin directory and the backend registers\n  it when Hermes loads the plugin, so it resolves as\n  `tiiny-router:tiiny-model-routing`. There is no separate copy under\n  `~/.hermes/skills/`.\n- **Models already downloaded.** The skill never downloads anything. It\n  chooses and loads from what is already on the device, and a model that is\n  not downloaded cannot be loaded — the plan will say so and pick something\n  else. Download models yourself with `tiiny download <model>`.\n\n## The two halves\n\n| | What it is | What it owns |\n|---|---|---|\n| **The router** (`plugin/`) | A delegation backend. Hermes calls it once per subtask. | Choosing the model for each subtask, and its reasoning mode. |\n| **The controller** (`scripts/tiiny_ctl.py`) | A command-line tool the manager agent drives. | The plan, the lock, the load, the restore, the catalogue refresh. |\n\nThe split is not tidiness. The delegation plugin has exactly one hook —\n`route_task`, called once per subtask *before* any child runs — and there is\nno \"batch finished\" hook. A plugin therefore cannot know when it is safe to\nevict a model, and cannot know whether a sibling agent is already using the\ndevice. Loading has to be a step the manager agent takes and can see.\n\nThe two halves meet at one file, the **plan** (`~/.hermes/tiiny-plan.json`).\nThe controller writes it when it plans the batch; the router reads it when it\nroutes each subtask. That is how a specialist the controller just loaded\nbecomes the specialist the child actually runs on.\n\n## The lifecycle\n\n```\n  1  evaluate     what kind of work is this?\n  2  pick         which model fits it?\n  3  fit          do the models for the whole batch fit in one NPU pool?\n  4  reserve      is the device free, and is it ours for the duration?\n  5  load         put the batch's models on the device\n  6  plan         one wave at a time — the device serves one stream\n  7  supervise    watch the child, do not lose the thread\n  8  verify       check the artifact, not the summary\n  9  release      put the device back and let other agents in\n 10  refresh      check the Model Store for models worth adding\n```\n\nEach step has a checkable completion criterion. Do not skip ahead — a skipped\nstep here fails quietly, and a quiet failure on this device looks exactly\nlike success.\n\n## Pointing the router at the mesh\n\nThe router is single-URL by design. Point that URL at the mesh and it\nbecomes multi-target: residency and the chat request both come from the\ngateway, which picks the box. Four changes, all in the plugin and the\nHermes config — no mesh change:\n\n1. **URL** — `TIINY_BASE_URL` in `tiers.py` prefers `TIINY_MESH_URL` when\n   set, then `TIINY_BASE_URL`, then the device hostname. Set\n   `TIINY_MESH_URL=http://127.0.0.1:8767/v1` in `~/.hermes/.env`.\n2. **Key** — the mesh authenticates with its **gateway key**\n   (`~/.hermes/mesh/gateway_key`), and rejects device bearers with 401.\n   `device_key()` must return the gateway key when the URL is the mesh,\n   and the device key otherwise. Detect by URL (`127.0.0.1:8767`), not by\n   guessing from the key's shape.\n3. **Id normalisation** — `/v1/models` on the mesh returns qualified ids\n   (`tiiny-ant/Qwen/Qwen3.6-35B-A3B`) plus a bare `model_id` field. The\n   tier table holds bare ids. Record **both** in `resident_models()`, or\n   every tier reads as \"not loaded\" and the router silently degrades to\n   the generalist.\n4. **Provider** — add a `tiinymesh` provider block (`api` =\n   mesh URL, `key_env: TIINY_MESH_KEY`, `transport: chat_completions`) and\n   set `delegation.provider: custom:tiinymesh`. The route config is passed\n   through unchanged, so the provider in the delegation section is the\n   provider the child runs on.\n\n**The mesh accepts bare ids for chat** — verified HTTP 200 — so chat needs\nno id rewriting; only the residency match does. A downloaded-but-not-\nresident model auto-loads on first request (~35–40s), so a slow first call\nis not a failure.\n\n**Editing `~/.hermes/config.yaml` is blocked for the patch tool.** It is\nsecurity-sensitive: the tool refuses. Edit it with a Python heredoc in\n`terminal` (back up first) or use `hermes config set`, then validate with\n`hermes config get delegation.provider` and `hermes config get\nproviders.tiinymesh` — `yaml` is not importable from the system Python,\nso do not validate by importing it.\n\n**Changes to `.env` and `config.yaml` are read at server start.** A\nrunning session keeps the old values and reports \"No API key for custom\nprovider … not set for this profile\" even though `hermes config get`\nreturns the key. Restart the app after the edits; verify the whole path in\na fresh process first:\n\n```bash\nhermes -z \"Reply with exactly: MESHOK\" \\\n  -m \"Qwen/Qwen3.6-35B-A3B\" --provider custom:tiinymesh\n```\n\nA `MESHOK` answer proves provider resolution, key lookup and mesh chat. A\ndelegation error in the already-running session afterwards is the stale\nprocess, not a broken config.\n\n## Quick Reference\n\n### The tiers\n\n| Tier | Model | NPU | Context | Reasoning | On the device? |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| `research` | `InternScience/Sage-A1.5-Turbo` | 48% | 262144 | switch | downloaded |\n| `research` (alt) | `endless-frontier/BigBang-v1-Turbo` | 54% | 131072 | switch | downloaded |\n| `index` | `openai/gpt-oss-20b` | 27% | 131072 | **levels only** | **resident** |\n| `code` | `Qwen/Qwen3.6-35B-A3B` | 47% | 262144 | switch | **resident** |\n| `code` (alt) | `ornith-ai/Ornith-1.5-35B-A3B-Turbo` | 54% | 262144 | switch | downloaded |\n| `code` (alt) | `Qwen/Qwen3-Coder-30B-A3B-Instruct-Turbo` | 55% | 65536 | none | downloaded |\n| `code` (heavy) | `Qwen/Qwen3-Coder-Next` | 94% | 131072 | none | not downloaded |\n| `browser` | `Qwen/Qwen3.8-27B` | 52% | 262144 | switch | downloaded |\n| `draft` | `Qwen/Qwen3.5-9B` | 29% | 131072 | switch | downloaded |\n| `general` | `Qwen/Qwen3.6-35B-A3B` | 47% | 262144 | switch | **resident** |\n\nThe Reasoning column is a snapshot of what the device reported on 2026-11-09.\n**The device is the authority** — the router reads the capability at route\ntime, so this table can go stale without breaking anything. See\n[Thinking mode](#thinking-mode).\n\n### The commands\n\n| Command | What it does |\n|---|---|\n| `tiiny_ctl.py status` | Device, resident set with NPU shares, the lock, the hold, the plan |\n| `tiiny_ctl.py plan --goal \"...\"` | Pick the models, check the NPU fit, write the plan |\n| `tiiny_ctl.py reserve --owner NAME` | Take the cross-agent lock and announce it |\n| `tiiny_ctl.py load [--wave N]` | Load the batch's models, evict what does not fit, verify |\n| `tiiny_ctl.py ensure --model M` | Make a model resident on demand — run this before delegating |\n| `tiiny_ctl.py release` | Restore the required set, clear the lock, the plan and the notice |\n| `tiiny_ctl.py catalog --refresh` | Diff the live Model Store against the last snapshot |\n| `tiiny_ctl.py image \"a job\"` | Pick the right image model for a job |\n| `route_check.py \"a goal\"` | Read-only: which model would this goal route to, and why |\n| `route_check.py --thinking off \"a goal\"` | The same, plus the reasoning state it would achieve |\n\n## Picking a model for a project role\n\nThe tiers answer one sub-task at a time. A project — a requirements doc to be\nplanned and then run — has three roles (planner, manager, worker) and they are\nnot chosen the same way: the planner is bought for depth per token, the manager\nfor state retention across turns, the worker for specialisation. The decision\ntable, the discriminators and the two-box topology are in\n[references/project-role-selection.md](references/project-role-selection.md).\n\nTwo cases the tiers do not cover and that reference does:\n\n- **A manager is not a reasoning role.** It is a state-retention role, so\n  thinking preservation and reliable tool calling beat raw reasoning depth.\n- **A 100% NPU model is escalation, not a standing resident** — load it for\n  the one hard decision and unload it, or nothing else fits beside it.\n\n## Procedure\n\n### 1 — Evaluate the job\n\nName the shape honestly. \"Index every file under X and classify each\" is an\nindexing task; \"read these logs and tell me what broke\" is a drafting task. A\ngoal that understates the work gets the generalist, which is the wrong model\nfor it.\n\nThe router infers the shape from the goal text, and the `tier` parameter\noverrides it. Use the override where inference would guess wrong — an\nunknown tier is refused loudly rather than silently mapped.\n\n*Done when:* each goal states the operation, not just the subject.\n\n### 2 — Pick the model, and check it can run\n\n```bash\npython3 scripts/tiiny_ctl.py plan --owner \"$AGENT\" --task \"what this batch is for\" \\\n  --goal \"index every file under the vault and classify each\" \\\n  --goal \"research the latest on agent memory systems\"\n```\n\nThe plan prints, per goal: the tier, the model, its NPU share, the wave it\nbelongs to, and whether the model is resident or merely downloaded. A model\nthat is **not downloaded** cannot be loaded — the plan falls back to a\ncandidate that can, and says so.\n\n*Done when:* every goal has a model that is on the device.\n\n### 3 — Ensure the combination fits\n\nOne shared NPU pool, and a load is a **swap**: it evicts whatever was\nresident. Models over 100% cannot be resident together — a 47% model plus a\n54% model is 101%.\n\nThe plan packs the batch into **waves**: each wave's models fit inside 100%,\nand a wave that does not fit with the rest is a second delegation call. The\nplan also prints what the wave will displace and what stays alongside it.\n\n*Done when:* every wave's NPU sum is 100% or less.\n\n### 4 — Reserve the device\n\n```bash\npython3 scripts/tiiny_ctl.py reserve --owner \"$AGENT\" --task \"what this batch is for\"\n```\n\nThis takes the lock and writes a notice into the vault's `hot.md` so other\nagents in the AMC can see that Tiiny is spoken for. If the device is already\nheld, the reserve **refuses** and tells you who holds it and until when.\n\n*Done when:* the lock is held and the notice is in `hot.md`.\n\n### 5 — Load the batch's models\n\n```bash\npython3 scripts/tiiny_ctl.py load --wave 1     # then --wave 2, and so on\n```\n\nThe controller writes the hold first — so `status` reports what the batch is\nabout to have — then decides what to evict, then loads, then **verifies that\nevery model in the wave is resident**. A model that did not land cannot\nanswer — the device returns `404 \"is not loaded\"` and the child falls back\nto the paid provider, invisibly.\n\n*Done when:* the load reports no model missing.\n\n### 6 — Plan the work: the device is single-threaded\n\n**Tiiny serves one generation-heavy stream at a time.** Two heavy children\nstarve each other, and the failure mode is a *hang*, not an error — the\ndevice keeps reporting healthy while it answers nothing.\n\nSo: run **one heavy child per wave**, not every wave's children at once. The\nparent's own cloud route is a separate stream, so parent work and one device\nchild genuinely run in parallel. If a batch needs a second workstream, give\nit a named non-device provider.\n\n**A single `delegate_task` batch with two tasks is two heavy children.** The\nbatch API is the easiest way to break this rule by accident: one call, two\nentries, both generation-heavy, both pointed at the device. The symptom is\nnot an error — it is a hang that reads like slow progress. One child sat\n`waiting for model response` for over fifteen minutes while the device\nreported healthy, and its sibling stalled on the same pool. Dispatch the\nsecond child only after the first has returned.\n\n**A hung child may still have written its output.** The hang is often in the\nhandover, not the work: the child can finish writing the artifact and then\nstall trying to report it. When a child has run far past its expected\nduration, **look for its file on disk before concluding it produced nothing**\n— the artifact is the evidence, and the status line is not. Read what it\nwrote, then decide whether to keep it, merge it, or supersede it. Stop the\nchild either way, so it is not still holding the device.\n\n*Done when:* no wave runs more than one generation-heavy child at a time.\n\n### 7 — Supervise the child\n\nThe child is running on a small local model, so give it a job it can finish:\nstate the operation, the output, and where to write it. Ask it to save raw\nfindings to a file early and keep its summary short — that is what keeps a\nlocal model competitive with a frontier one.\n\nWatch for the two failure modes that look like progress: a child that is\nstalled behind the device's queue, and a child that fell back to the paid\nprovider. The child's route carries the backend and the label\n(`code→generalist` means the specialist was not available).\n\n*Done when:* the child has written its findings at least once, and its route\nlabel says what you expected.\n\n### 8 — Check the child's work\n\nA child's report is a self-report. Recompute the numbers it claims from the\nprimary record with a script, and render what it built — a delivered HTML\nfile is not done when it parses.\n\n*Done when:* a script recomputes every claimed count from the source and\nexits zero.\n\n### 9 — Release the device\n\n```bash\npython3 scripts/tiiny_ctl.py release\n```\n\nThis restores the required models from `REQUIRED_MODELS` — the single copy of\nthat policy, in `scripts/tiiny_ctl.py` (see \"The required set\") — clears the\nhold, clears the plan, releases the lock, and removes the notice from\n`hot.md`. **Restoring is\npart of the run, not a follow-up**: a swap that is not restored breaks every\nlater delegated child, and the failure looks like a model problem rather\nthan a leftover state.\n\n*Done when:* the required models are resident, the hold is gone, the lock is\nfree, and `hot.md` no longer carries the notice.\n\n### 10 — Keep the catalogue honest\n\n```bash\npython3 scripts/tiiny_ctl.py catalog --refresh\n```\n\nThis diffs the live Model Store against the last snapshot and reports what is\nnew, what is gone, and whose NPU share or context window moved. If a tier\nmodel's figures changed, the tier table in `plugin/tiers.py` is now wrong —\nupdate it, and update\n[`tiiny-discovery/tiiny-model-catalogue.md`](../../Tiiny/model-catalogue.md)\nto match.\n\nThe snapshot records **facts**; the analysis around them is written by hand.\nNew models are worth adding to the tier table when they are genuinely better\nat a shape the tiers do not cover — not because they are new.\n\nWhen you need a publication-ready catalogue with live `desc` + measured overlay, build it reproducibly: `curl -H \"Authorization: Bearer $TIINY_API_KEY\" http://tiinyhost.local/api/v1/models/online_models` to snapshot all 92, `cp app/data/overlay.yaml /tmp/overlay.yaml` first (the Hermes gateway refuses to open vault/iCloud paths directly — copy to `/tmp` before parsing), then merge `thinking` + `overlay.yaml` measured fields (`image_input`, `alpha`, `text_rendering`) via a script (see `/tmp/build_catalog_doc.py`) and write the MD to `~/SatanDocuments/` and a vault copy via `cp /tmp/*.md` — never hand-edit a drifted NPU/context cell.\n\n*Done when:* the diff is empty, or the differences are reflected in the tier\ntable and the catalogue.\n\n## Thinking mode\n\nA reasoning model answers in two parts: the trace, and the answer. The trace\nis not free, and on mechanical work it is not wanted. Indexing a folder does\nnot need a chain of thought; it needs the folder.\n\nSo each task carries a `thinking` parameter:\n\n| Value | What happens |\n|---|---|\n| `auto` (default) | Nothing is applied. The model keeps its native behaviour. |\n| `off` | Reasoning is turned off where the model can be turned off, and down to the model's cheapest reasoning level where it cannot. |\n| `on` | Reasoning is turned on where the model can be turned on, and up to its most expensive level where it cannot. |\n\n```bash\npython3 scripts/route_check.py --thinking off \"index every file under the vault\"\n```\n\n### The device knows more than a table does\n\nModels differ in *how* reasoning is controlled, and the differences are not\nguessable:\n\n- Some models have a **switch** — `enable_thinking: false` stops the trace\n  dead.\n- Some models have only **levels** — `low`, `medium`, `high` — and their\n  switch is ignored outright.\n- Some models **do not reason at all**, and a request to turn reasoning on is\n  not a thing that can happen.\n- One model is flagged by the device as having no switch and honours it\n  perfectly well. **The flag is a hint, not ground truth.**\n\nSo the router does not keep a table of this. It reads the device's own\ncapability for the model it picked, at route time, and applies the control\nthat model actually honours:\n\n```json\n{\"thinking\": {\"supported\": true, \"toggleable\": false, \"levels\": [\"low\", \"medium\", \"high\"]}}\n```\n\nThat comes from `GET /v1/models` for a resident model and\n`GET /api/v1/models/online_models` for the whole store. The Model Store\nchanges with every TiinyOS update, and a hand-maintained capability table\ngoes stale quietly — which is worse than having none, because it is\nbelieved.\n\n### \"Off\" means the cheapest available state\n\nFor a model with no working switch, `thinking: \"off\"` is **not a refusal and\nnot a pretence**. It means the cheapest state the model offers: the lowest\nreasoning level it declares. The model still reasons — it just reasons less —\nand the label says so rather than claiming a switch that does not exist.\n\n### The label reports what happened, not what was asked\n\nEvery route carries a label. The thinking state is part of it:\n\n| Label | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| `[think:off]` | The switch worked. |\n| `[think:off→low]` | The switch was ignored; the model is at its cheapest level. |\n| `[think:on]` | The switch worked. |\n| `[think:on→high]` | The switch was ignored; the model is at its most expensive level. |\n| `[think:unsupported]` | The model does not reason at all. |\n| `[think:unknown]` | The device would not say, so nothing was applied. |\n| `[think:auto]` | Nothing was applied; native behaviour. |\n\nA route that asked for `off` and got `off→low` did not turn reasoning off.\nSaying otherwise is how a token bill becomes a surprise.\n\n### Thinking on needs a token floor\n\nA reasoning model spends its budget on the trace first. With a small\n`max_tokens`, the trace eats the budget and the request comes back **HTTP 200\nwith an empty answer** — a silent failure that looks like the model produced\nnothing.\n\nSo the router applies an output floor (4096, the budget children already run\nwith) whenever a model's reasoning is in play. If you raise your own budget\nabove that, raise the floor with it, or a reasoning child is quietly clamped\nback down:\n\n```yaml\ndelegation:\n  backends:\n    tiiny-router:\n      thinking_max_tokens: 8192\n```\n\n## The image benefit\n\nThe same device runs the image models, and picking the right one is the\ndifference between an image that works and an afternoon. The controller picks\nfrom what is on the device:\n\n```bash\npython3 scripts/tiiny_ctl.py image \"a realistic portrait of an engineer\"\npython3 scripts/tiiny_ctl.py image \"a logo with the word ARC in it\"\npython3 scripts/tiiny_ctl.py image \"a character turnaround, three views\"\n```\n\n| The job | The model | NPU |\n|---|---|---|\n| Photorealism, legible text, fast iteration | `Tongyi-MAI/Z-Image-Turbo` | 26% |\n| Maximum fidelity — fur, foliage, reflections | `HiDream-ai/HiDream-O1-Image-Dev` | 30% |\n| A realistic person — skin, hair, fabric | `TaoBaiBai2025/Z-Image-Turbo-Creating-Realistic` | 26% |\n| A character turnaround in one image | `YeiYeiArt/Z-Image-Turbo-CharacterDesignSheet` | 26% |\n| Cut a subject out of an existing photo | `fal/FLUX.2-klein-4B-Background-Remove` | 26% |\n\nAn image model evicts the chat models — the NPU is one pool — so image work\nbelongs inside a reserved batch, and the release puts everything back. See\n[references/image-models.md](references/image-models.md) for the sizes that\nwork and the ones that do not.\n\n## The lock\n\nTwo agents delegating at once will fight over the same models and the same\nsilicon. The lock makes that visible instead of silent.\n\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| **The mutex** | `~/.hermes/tiiny-lock.json`. Taken under `flock`, so two agents cannot both believe they hold it. It **expires**, so a crashed agent does not lock the device overnight. |\n| **The announcement** | A marked block in the vault's `hot.md` — the AMC's own board of what is in flight — so agents that are not Hermes profiles can see the device is spoken for. |\n\n`status` shows both. `release` clears both.\n\n**The device cannot record who loaded what.** Its management API does expose\nload and unload routes, but they report only the model and the outcome — no\nactor and no owner — so there is still no server-side audit trail to read, and\na usage log has to be written on this side, by the tool that performs the load.\nThat makes the controller the only writer that can be trusted: a log kept by\nconvention is bypassed by every bare `tiiny load`, and a bypassed log is\nworse than no log because it claims completeness it does not have.\n\n## The required set\n\n**There is no watchdog any more.** A five-minute cron poller used to reload a\nfixed set of models whenever the device looked cold. It was retired for two\nreasons: that set was duplicated across three scripts and drifted apart three\ntimes, and a fixed set is the wrong shape when a batch needs one specific\nmodel.\n\n**Prove that, do not assume it — and do not trust a document that still says a\nwatchdog exists.** The four places a timer can hide, all of them cheap to read:\n`crontab -l`; `~/Library/LaunchAgents`; `/Library/LaunchDaemons`; and the\nHermes job store at `~/.hermes/cron/jobs.json` (there is one hourly job that\nmentions \"watchdog\" — the Engraphis MCP watchdog — and it restarts a server,\nnot a model). A vault `AGENTS.md` that describes a five-minute reload poller\nfighting a deliberate swap is stale; the mechanism is gone, and quoting it back\nto the user as a live risk is a wrong answer.\n\n**The mesh gateway has a restore path, and it is inert by design.**\n`_restore_worker` in `mesh/gateway.py` fires **once at gateway start**, retries\n6 × 20 s while the boxes come up, and only ever *loads* — there is no unload\ncode and no timer. It is gated by `auto_restore` in `~/.hermes/mesh/config.json`,\nand the log prints `[restore] disabled by config (auto_restore=false)` at every\nstart. Note what that switch really controls: it turns off `keep_resident` too,\nso on a box that declares one, a reboot leaves it cold until `ensure` runs.\nThat is the deliberate trade — no mid-batch eviction, no unattended recovery —\nand it should be stated as a trade rather than fixed by turning restore on.\n\n**What does evict a model mid-project is traffic, not time.** Two paths, both\nin the mesh: the `borrow`/`unborrow` pair (`mesh.py`) snapshots `was_resident`,\nloads an on-demand model on a Tiiny, serves, then restores — during that window\nthe resident specialist is gone; and any route whose `policy` entry points at a\nTiiny will trigger it. Read `policy` before a long run: an `image_gen` primary\nof `tiiny-octo`, an `embedding` secondary of `tiiny-ant/...`, or a `chat`\nsecondary on the other box are each an eviction waiting for one request.\n\n- **`ensure` replaces it.** Before delegating, make the model resident:\n\n```bash\npython3 scripts/tiiny_ctl.py ensure --model \"Qwen/Qwen3.6-35B-A3B\"\n```\n\nIt is idempotent, it checks the cross-agent lock, it evicts explicitly when a\nmodel does not fit, and it exits non-zero when a model did not land. The two\nscan jobs run it before they dispatch a child — if `ensure` fails, they do not\ndelegate, because a missing model means a 404 and a silent fallback to the\npaid provider.\n\n**The router calls `ensure` automatically.** When `ENSURE_ON_ROUTE` is `True`\n(the default), `route_task` calls `ctl.ensure_model(model, quiet=True)` after\nchoosing the model and before returning the route. This makes the device ready\nfor the child without an extra manual step. The call is idempotent — when the\nmodel is already running, it costs one HTTP round-trip. When it is not, the\ncontroller loads it and verifies the load before returning. Exceptions are\nsilently caught so a load failure cannot break routing — the device returns 404\nand Hermes falls back to the paid provider as expected.\n\nTo disable, set the environment variable ``TIINY_ENSURE_ON_ROUTE=false`` or\nedit ``ENSURE_ON_ROUTE`` in the plugin.\n\n**One list, one file.** `REQUIRED_MODELS` in `scripts/tiiny_ctl.py` is the\nonly copy. Every other location — `~/.hermes/scripts/`, both skill tiers,\n`~/.hermes/shared-skills/` — is a **symlink** into the canonical file in the\nvault, so a change cannot fork. Only the publish tree holds a second real\ncopy, because it ships to GitHub and git cannot publish a link; the deploy\nstep regenerates it. The mechanics — why each skill root still needs its own\ndirectory, why a symlink in a git tree shows as a typechange, and how to\nconvert an existing set of copies — are in\n[references/single-source-deployment.md](references/single-source-deployment.md).\n\n**Trimming is a functional decision, not just an NPU decision.** Hermes's own\n`stt` and `tts` providers used to point at the appliance, and TTS has no\non-demand load path — a TTS model that is not resident makes `text_to_speech`\nfail. That constraint is gone: speech now lives on the Mac's OffGrid gateway,\nwhich serves it from its own process at no NPU cost. See\n`mlops/offgrid-local-inference`. The appliance's speech models were unloaded\non 2026-11-10 and are no longer in the required set.\n\n- **Two boxes need a per-box capability map, not one table.** The two boxes carry\n  the SAME store — verified 2026-11-13: identical 93 model ids, and identical\n  `npu_usage` / `context_window` / `type` / `capabilities` / `params` for every\n  one of them. They differ only in what is on disk and what is resident, so\n  \"which model do I ask for\" is a question about download state, never about\n  configuration. `references/two-box-capability-matrix.md` has the endpoint map,\n  the NPU-budget findings, and the classification rules; `scripts/tiiny_two_box_matrix.py`\n  regenerates and self-verifies the whole matrix read-only.\n- **The NPU budget is 48 GiB and only one endpoint says so.**\n  `GET /api/v1/npu/status` -> `devices[0].mem_total_mb` = 49024 (LQ50-48GB).\n  The catalogue's `npu_usage` is percent of that pool (resident declares 47,\n  device measures 46.30%), so a co-resident set must sum to <= 100.\n  `GET /api/v1/sys/status` -> `npus[].memory_total_mb` is NOT the budget — it\n  moves between samples and never equals 49024. Every other `/api/v1/npu/*`\n  path 404s.\n- **The device has no code flag, and a naive \"does the desc contain 'coding'\"\n  misfires.** The word appears as a purpose (\"is a coding-specialized model\"),\n  as a training-data footnote (gpt-oss-20b/120b) and as a benchmark aside\n  (Qwen3.5-9B). Only the first is a capability. See\n  `references/two-box-capability-matrix.md` for the rule and the models it\n  separates.\n\n`references/required-set-drift.md` keeps the audit recipe for \"who changed the\nresident set, and when\" — still worth reading, because the mechanism that\ncaused those drifts (one policy, many copies) is exactly what the symlinks\nnow make impossible.\n\n## Scheduled work on the device\n\nDelegated children are not the only thing that can run on the box — a\nscheduled job can, and a scheduled job is the better host for work that has\nto happen unattended every day. The two differ in one way that matters:\n**a cron job's provider is pinned per job**, so a box that is down fails\nthe job instead of quietly falling through to a paid model.\n\n### Pin a job to the box\n\n```bash\nhermes cron edit <job_id> --model \"Qwen/Qwen3.6-35B-A3B\" --provider custom:tiiny --pin\n```\n\nPrecedence is **per-job pin > `cron.model_provider` > the persisted global\nconfig**, and the model follows the same order (`cron.model` > the main\nagent model). Set the defaults once and a job created later starts on the\nbox without anyone pinning it:\n\n```bash\nhermes config set cron.model \"Qwen/Qwen3.6-35B-A3B\"\nhermes config set cron.model_provider custom:tiiny\n```\n\n### The pin is what makes the saving real\n\nA job that carries its own `model`, `provider` or `base_url` gets **no\nfallback chain at all** — it does not inherit `fallback_providers`, so a box\nthat is down produces a **failed job with a visible error** and a zero bill.\nAn unpinned job inherits the chain and can quietly spend. Pin the job, and\nthe failure is legible instead of expensive.\n\n### Do not route scheduled work through the mesh\n\nThe mesh is a router, and a router's **secondary is a paid provider**. A\nchat policy whose secondary is `openrouter/...` falls through to it the\nmoment the box misses — so the saving disappears exactly when the box is\nbusy. **Point scheduled work at a device provider block directly.** It is\nalso materially faster: a live call measured ~2.4 s direct against ~34 s\nthrough the mesh.\n\n### Look for the provider block before you write one\n\n`providers:` usually already carries a block per box — `tiiny`, `tiiny2`,\n`tiinymesh` — each complete with its own `key_env` and model list. Grep\nthe config before writing a block: one that already exists needs no edit\nand no restart, and a **new** block is not live until the client restarts,\nbecause provider blocks are read at startup.\n\n### Contention is decided by agent windows, not by the clock\n\nA scheduled job's **script phase does no model work** — the script collects\ndata and the agent interprets it. Only the agent phase occupies the device.\nSo two jobs whose schedules are minutes apart may not contend at all, and\ntwo jobs hours apart certainly can. Place jobs by when each one's **agent**\nphase runs, and give the longest agent phase a box to itself: two boxes are\ntwo free streams.\n\n### The cron inactivity watchdog\n\n`HERMES_CRON_TIMEOUT` is the limit on how long a job may be **silent**\nbefore the scheduler declares it dead. It is **not** a cap on total\nruntime. The default is 600 s, sized for a provider that answers in\nseconds; a local model on a long context can be quiet for a minute or two\nbetween turns. Raise it when jobs start dying at the watchdog rather than\nat the model. Both the inactivity monitor and the cwd-lock bound derive\nfrom this one value, so they cannot drift apart.\n\n`~/.hermes/.env` is a protected credential file with no CLI of its own —\nhand the human a script for it (see the pitfall below).\n\n## Pitfalls\n\n- **A load is a swap.** Loading a model evicts the resident one; the evicted\n  model's endpoint then returns `404`, which means *not resident*, not a bad\n  model id. Restore the required set when the work is done.\n- **The device decides what to evict — unless you decide first.** Left alone\n  it kept the 47% generalist and evicted the 27% model the batch had just\n  asked for. The controller evicts explicitly, keeping the cheapest models\n  that fit. Never let a bare `tiiny load` be the only thing that touches the\n  device during a batch.\n- **A cold device bills you, and says nothing.** The appliance unloads on\n  reboot. There is no poller putting models back any more, so the *parent*\n  must run `ensure` before it delegates. Skip that and the child gets a 404\n  which Hermes classifies as terminal and answers from the paid provider.\n  Nothing on a timer unloads a model either — an eviction comes from another\n  load, which in practice means a mesh `policy` route pointing at the wrong\n  box. Read `policy` before a long run, not the clock.\n\n- **A logged-out CLI session looks exactly like a cold device, and it is\n  the more dangerous of the two.** `tiiny load` opens a device-admin session.\n  With no session it fails with \"Not logged in. Use 'tiiny login' to sign\n  in\", nothing becomes resident, and `ensure` has to notice. The load verdict\n  therefore never claims a model was loaded when it was not: it prints\n  `NOT RESIDENT` on its own, never `loaded: X` next to `NOT RESIDENT: X`.\n  A batch that reads only the exit code and skips the log still catches it —\n  the exit code is non-zero. Check the session before blaming the NPU.\n\n- **The catalogue is the device's, not the table's.** Read `GET\n  /api/v1/models/online_models` and use each entry's `npu_usage` and\n  `context_window`. When two boxes are in play, compare their catalogues\n  against each other rather than assuming one table fits both: measured\n  2026-11-12, the Pocket and the Octo reported identical model sets and\n  identical NPU and context figures for every tier model, while stocking\n  them differently (16 models on one, 42 on the other). That is what makes\n  one box the pinned vault and the other the flex box.\n\n- **Two boxes need two locks, two holds and two plans — and one endpoint\n  each.** The shared state (lock, hold, plan, catalogue snapshot) is\n  device-scoped through `TIINY_LOCK_FILE`, `TIINY_HOLD_FILE`,\n  `TIINY_PLAN_FILE` and `TIINY_SNAPSHOT_FILE`, and the endpoints through\n  `TIINY_HOST` and `TIINY_BASE_URL`. The defaults are the single-device\n  paths, so nothing moves unless a caller asks. The alias\n  `openai.api.tiiny` resolves to the desktop app's forwarder and serves ONE\n  device at a time — a second box has to be addressed by its own IP.\n\n- **The controller drives ONE box; the mesh lifecycle route drives both.**\n  `tiiny_ctl.py` reads the bearer from the literal name `TIINY_API_KEY` only\n  (there is no `TIINY_API_KEY_2` fallback), one `MANAGEMENT_BASE` from\n  `TIINY_HOST`, and shells its load/unload out to `tiiny load`, whose session\n  is bound to a single device. To reach the second box with it you must export\n  that box's key **as** `TIINY_API_KEY` in that shell, plus its `TIINY_HOST`\n  and its own lock/hold/plan/snapshot files. For both boxes at once, use\n  `POST /mesh/lifecycle` `{action, model, target}` on the mesh gateway, or the\n  box's own `POST /api/v1/models/<id>/start|stop` with its bearer — both run\n  concurrently, no CLI session involved. Recipe and the per-box error\n  asymmetry: [references/two-box-capability-matrix.md](references/two-box-capability-matrix.md#driving-both-boxes-at-once).\n\n- **Downloading a model is a separate path from loading one.** `POST\n  /api/v1/models/<id>/download` pulls from the store onto the device's disk,\n  authorised with the box's bearer and needing no CLI session; `tiiny download\n  <model_id> --device-address <ip>` does the same thing through the CLI, where\n  it deadlocks after the transfer and always exits non-zero. A download\n  costs no NPU — the model sits on disk until something loads it, which is\n  where the swap budget applies. `tiiny ls -r` is the store catalogue,\n  `tiiny ls -d` is what is on disk, and the difference is the download list.\n  `tiiny import` covers models the store does not carry.\n\n- **The CLI's exit code, and its output after a transfer, are not the\n  truth.** A completed download dies with `fatal error: all goroutines are\n  asleep - deadlock!` — the command's own progress reporter parks on a\n  channel that never signals, and the process exits non-zero every time.\n  The \"already downloaded\" path exits non-zero with a perfectly normal\n  sentence. Verify every download against the device's own catalogue entry\n  — `status`, `progress`, and the byte count — not against the CLI's exit\n  code. Same rule as the load verdict: the device decides.\n\n- **`tiiny load` and `tiiny unload` need a real terminal.** In a\n  non-interactive shell they fail with \"could not open a new TTY\" and\n  nothing becomes resident — a silent no-op that looks like a refusal to\n  load. Run them inside a pseudo-terminal (`script -q /dev/null tiiny load\n  ...`) when there is no TTY. The appliance exposes no SSH shell to borrow\n  one from — ports 22 and 2222 are closed on both boxes, and the device's\n  36-route API has no shell, exec or console route — and `--json` does not\n  skip the prompt. Any controlling terminal works: the `script` wrapper, or\n  a runner that allocates one.\n\n- **The control CLI holds ONE session, bound to ONE device.** Signing in\n  for a second box signs the first one *out* — the stored session is a\n  single opaque token, not a map of devices. The CLI can therefore manage\n  one box at a time, and moving the session between boxes is a human step\n  (a sign-in with a code). Two rules follow: every control call has to\n  name its device — `tiiny_ctl.py` resolves `TIINY_DEVICE_ADDRESS`, then\n  `TIINY_HOST`, then the CLI config, and passes `--device-address` to\n  every load and unload — and a controller aimed at a box that does not\n  hold the session must say so, rather than failing as though the model\n  were the problem. The API credentials are unaffected: each box's bearer\n  is independent, and both boxes serve inference at the same time.\n\n- **A script that edits a credentials file must write atomically.** A\n  mistyped `open(path, \"w\", encoding=...)` truncated the file before it\n  raised. Write through a temp file and `os.replace` it into place, and\n  prove the change against a copy — the real config is the one file that\n  cannot be rebuilt from a backup of itself.\n\n- **The client config is off-limits to the agent; hand the human a script.**\n  `~/.hermes/config.yaml` is guarded by design, and the right response is\n  not to route around the guard: write the change as a script the human\n  runs — a timestamped backup, one line edited, a temp file and `os.replace`\n  into place, a printed revert command, and a proof run against a COPY of\n  the config first. The human restarts the client afterwards anyway, and a\n  provider block is config they need to see. **A provider block is read at\n  startup, so a newly added provider is not live until the client\n  restarts** — say that when the change is handed over, because a provider\n  that appears configured but answers nothing looks like a routing fault.\n  `hermes config set KEY VAL` covers `config.yaml`; `~/.hermes/.env` has no\n  CLI of its own, so a credential-file change is always the hand-run\n  script.\n- **A revert must remove what you added, not rewind the file.** The newest\n  backup of a file you have already edited still contains your own change,\n  so restoring it does not revert anything — it just restores your last\n  state. Revert by removing the one key you added, leaving every other\n  byte alone, and keep the backup for the case where the file itself is\n  wrong.\n\n- **Different form factors mean different NPU figures.** A Pocket and an\n  Octo are not the same silicon. The tier table's shares were measured on\n  one of them; re-measure the other (`catalog --refresh` pointed at it)\n  before routing anything to it.\n\n- **Do not hard-code a device's error strings.** A signature-based LAN scan\n  that matched on `{\"error\":\"auth_failed\"}` found nothing after the device's\n  OS update renamed it to `{\"error\":\"unauthorized\",\"reason\":\"missing_token\"}`.\n  Match on the route and the status code, and read the body for a human —\n  never a pinned string.\n\n- **A device's display name is not its identity.** A Tiiny was \"Tiiny\n  Pocket\" until it was renamed \"TinyAnt\" in the desktop app, and the CLI\n  scan, the device's own `device_name` and the account view each showed a\n  different string. Key every record, lock, plan and provider on the\n  **device ID** (`TNYM…`) and treat the name as a label.\n\n- **Each box has its own device-admin bearer, and the alias fronts only\n  one of them.** `tiiny auth key --device-address <ip>` issues a credential\n  for a specific box; the account-level key opens one box's store and\n  inference while the other answers \"Not authenticated\" on the same\n  routes. The alias `openai.api.tiiny` is a local nginx forwarder that\n  holds the credentials and serves the app's currently selected box — one\n  at a time. That is not a hardware limit: the boxes are separate hosts\n  with separate NPUs, and one answers while the other refuses. Two-box\n  parallelism needs two bearers and two direct addresses.\n\n- **A device stays closed until the desktop TiinyOS app has an\n  authenticated session for it.** The gate is the app's session, not the\n  bearer: with no app session the CLI and the API both refuse, and the\n  routes answer 401 / \"Not authenticated\" even with a valid key. One box\n  can be open while its sibling is closed. The CLI session is separate\n  again — `tiiny login` signs the CLI in, and it is what `tiiny load` and\n  `tiiny auth key` need.\n\n- **The CLI session is bound to the device it was signed in for.** Point it\n  at another box and it reports \"Not logged in\" even though the account\n  session is live — `tiiny auth key --device-address <ip>` issues a\n  credential for that box only to a session bound to that box. So each box\n  needs its own `tiiny login`, and each has its own bearer: two valid keys\n  existed for one box while neither opened the other. The alias rejects a\n  client-supplied bearer outright (401 even with a valid device key) — it\n  is app-internal, not an endpoint an agent can use.\n\n- **The alias is the app's forwarder, so address the box.** `openai.api.tiiny`\n  resolves to the desktop app's forwarder on 127.0.0.1, and that forwarder\n  follows the app's session and its currently selected device — it answers\n  for one box at a time and 401s whenever the session does not cover it. A\n  device is addressed by its own IP. The cost is that an IP needs a DHCP\n  reservation, because a provider pinned to a wandering address fails\n  silently as a fallback to the paid provider.\n\n- **Serving and loading are different paths.** A model that is already\n  running answers immediately — no CLI session needed. The CLI session is\n  needed only by the CLI, and the device's HTTP API does load, unload and\n  download without one. So a box with a warm model is usable while the CLI\n  is logged out, and a box that is *not* the signed-in one can still be\n  loaded, unloaded and stocked.\n\n- **The device's published route map is not proof of what it will accept.**\n  `GET /openapi.json` lists 36 paths and none of them is model lifecycle —\n  and the device still answers `POST /api/v1/models/<id>/download`,\n  `/start` and `/stop`. **A spec describes the system; the system decides\n  what it accepts.** Never write \"the device has no route for X\" from a\n  route map — POST it and read the status code. Probing an undocumented\n  route costs one request; publishing the absence costs a correction, and a\n  published correction stays in the commit history permanently.\n\n- **Three things decide which box a CLI command reaches: the flag, the\n  config, and the token — and they do not agree by default.**\n  `--device-address <ip>` reaches the box you named even when the config\n  points elsewhere; with no flag the CLI follows the config's\n  `deviceAddress`; and the stored token decides whether you are authorised\n  for whichever box is selected. So the flag **selects** the target and the\n  token **grants** access, and a session for one box will not open the\n  other however you address it. Pass the address on every control call, and\n  read a \"Not logged in\" answer as \"this token is for another box\", not as\n  \"the device is down\".\n\n- **The store entry is the download truth, not a storage endpoint.**\n  `GET /api/v1/models/online_models` carries each model's `status`\n  (`running` / `downloaded` / `downloading` / `not_downloaded`),\n  `progress` and `npu_usage`. `/api/v1/sys/storage/llm` reported\n  `total_size: 0` while eight models were downloaded and one was running —\n  it is not a model-presence measure, and reading it as one invents a\n  missing-models finding.\n\n- **A revoked bearer fails as a 401, and a stale one fails quietly\n  enough.** When it was revoked, every call returned 401 `auth_failed`\n  while the controller still reported \"key: found\" — found in the\n  environment, not valid at the device. Check the device's answer, not the\n  key's presence. The bearer is **device-scoped**: each box issues its own\n  from `tiiny auth key --device-address <ip>`, and one box's bearer answers\n  401 on the sibling box's routes.\n- **Two heavy children deadlock.** Not an error — a hang, with the device\n  still reporting healthy. One heavy child at a time.\n- **Dead fallbacks crash the run.** When a local model times out or stalls,\n  Hermes aborts the connection and silently falls back to\n  `delegation.fallback_providers`. If that fallback provider is exhausted\n  (e.g., MiniMax returning HTTP 429), the run fails immediately. Ensure\n  fallback points to an active provider with working credits (e.g., OpenRouter).\n  The `/mesh/fallback` route on TiinyMesh flips both the mesh chat secondary and\n  Hermes's `delegation.fallback_providers` in lockstep between OpenRouter and MiniMax.\n- **OpenRouter authentication & CA certificates on macOS.** Standard\n  `urllib.request` on macOS Python fails with `CERTIFICATE_VERIFY_FAILED`\n  against OpenRouter unless the system CA store / `certifi` bundle is loaded\n  (as in `mesh._ssl_context()`). OpenRouter's `/api/v1/credits` strictly\n  requires a management key (`OPENROUTER_MANAGEMENT_KEY`) and returns 403 on\n  inference keys; use `GET /api/v1/key` to check key spend and caps when only\n  an inference key is configured. Active keys may live in `~/.hermes/auth.json`\n  under `credential_pool` even when `~/.hermes/.env` holds a stale key.\n- **Large context causes NPU PCIe HDMA timeouts on 30B models.**\n  `Qwen/Qwen3-Coder-30B-A3B-Instruct-Turbo` has a 65K context ceiling, but on\n  hardware when prompt context approaches ~44K+ tokens, the device driver\n  times out (`pcie_hdma_timeout`) and hangs without closing the socket. After\n  180 seconds with no tokens, Hermes's stale-call watchdog aborts the connection\n  and silently falls back to `fallback_providers` (e.g., MiniMax). Keep prompts\n  under 30K tokens on 30B models, or use a 256K model (`Qwen3.6-35B-A3B` or\n  `Qwen3.8-27B`) for large context.\n- **Goal keywords override model intent in delegation.** The router infers\n  tiers from goal text (\"execute\", \"code\", \"run\" -> `tier: code`). A goal\n  stating \"execute the sprint plan\" routes to the 65K Coder model instead of\n  the 256K generalist or planner. Pass `tier=\"general\"` or `tier=\"browser\"`\n  explicitly when the task needs long context over coding specialization.\n- **Concurrent loads trigger PCIe HDMA timeouts.** Sending overlapping\n  model-load or lifecycle commands can cause the device driver to hit\n  `pcie_hdma_timeout` (\"NPU PCIe HDMA transfer timed out\"), emptying\n  `devices: []` and evicting all models (`running: []`). Always serialize\n  model loads sequentially per box.\n- **`route_check.py` does not take a `--model` flag.** It infers the tier from\n  goal text or accepts `--tier <name>` or `--all`. Passing `--model` is an\n  unrecognized argument error. Preflight with `--tier` or the goal string.\n- **`tiiny ls` shows size, not residency.** Its NPU column is how much NPU a\n  model takes, not whether it is loaded. `tiiny_ctl.py status` is the\n  residency truth.\n- **A 404 is not a typo.** Before concluding a model id is wrong, check\n  residency. A model can be in the Store and not loaded.\n- **A FORCED tier overrides residency, and that is how work silently moves\n  to the paid provider.** The router's inference respects what is loaded;\n  `delegate_task(tier=...)` does not — it selects the tier's first choice\n  and sends it. If that model is not resident the device answers 404, Hermes\n  treats 404 as terminal, and the child falls back to the paid provider\n  *invisibly*, with a good answer and a bill. Measured 2026-11-13: forcing\n  `tier=\"code\"` selected `Qwen/Qwen3-Coder-30B-A3B-Instruct-Turbo`\n  (\"NOT loaded\") where inference alone had selected the resident\n  `Qwen/Qwen3.6-35B-A3B`. **Run `route_check.py` before every dispatch and\n  only force a tier it reports as loaded.** The tier is a hint; residency\n  is the constraint.\n- **Context window is a ceiling, not a suggestion.** A child that silently\n  truncates its context is usually routed to a model whose window is too\n  small for the task, not to a broken task.\n- **A level inside `chat_template_kwargs` is not the same knob as a level at\n  the top level of the request.** The chat-template one is read by some model\n  families and silently ignored by others — a model whose chat template has\n  no boolean switch reads the level only at the top level of the request.\n  Putting it in the wrong place looks configured and changes nothing.\n- **`enable_thinking: false` is mandatory on every Tiiny route.** It is the\n  provider's `extra_body` and it survives the route's own overrides only\n  where the route does not set its own thinking state. Without it, every\n  child reasons by default, on every task, including the mechanical ones.\n- **A reasoning model with a small `max_tokens` returns an empty response**\n  with the budget spent in the trace, and the status code is 200. The router\n  applies an output floor for this reason; if you set `max_tokens` yourself,\n  keep it above the floor.\n- **The device's `toggleable` flag has been wrong.** A model flagged as\n  having no on/off switch honours it anyway. Send the boolean whenever it can\n  be sent, and let the label report what actually took effect.\n- **A stale hold makes `status` lie.** With no poller to disable, a leftover\n  hold no longer breaks the device — it just makes `status` report a resident\n  set that is no longer true. `release` clears it and verifies.\n- **Trimming the device is not trimming the policy.** `release` restores\n  `REQUIRED_MODELS`, so unloading a model without editing that list means it\n  returns at the next release and the change looks like it never applied.\n  Edit the list first, then unload. See \"The required set\" above.\n- **One thinking model hides it in its prose.** `trohrbaugh/Qwen3.8-27B-heretic-ara-Turbo` reports `thinking.supported:true` (switch + levels `low/medium/xhigh`, 56% NPU) but its `desc` never mentions \"thinking\" or \"reasoning\" — read `thinking` from `GET /api/v1/models/online_models` at route time, not from the description, or you will burn a trace you never asked for. On 2026-11-10 this was the only one of 22 thinking models with hidden prose.\n- **Absolute paths and vault-local prefixes are publish-blockers.** The\n  publisher's scanner forbids absolute home paths and any vault-local\n  prefix — a catalogue footer that spells out a home directory, the vault\n  root, or the publish tree is a `PUBLISH-SAFETY` finding and blocks the\n  push. Write review locations as a plain relative name and scrub before\n  pushing; JSON must also scan `CLEAN` before it ships as\n  `data/catalogue.json`. This bullet once named the forbidden patterns\n  literally, which made the skill's own file fail the scan it describes.\n- **A dangling link inside a skill is usually drift, not a missing file.**\n  Two broken links in one deployed copy of this skill pointed at reference\n  documents the upstream source had since replaced or renamed — that copy\n  was an older revision nobody had re-synced. Patching the link text would\n  have left it diverging from upstream in every other way. **Sync the whole\n  skill instead, then fix what the sync breaks.** Two consequences worth\n  knowing before you start: the upstream copy's new files may carry a device\n  serial or a private LAN address that the publish scanner refuses, so the\n  sync needs a **build-time redaction transform** in the publisher (redact\n  the published bytes, never the source — the running controller needs the\n  real addresses); and a deployed copy may hold files upstream does not\n  have, so a wholesale copy over it deletes working code. Diff before\n  overwriting, and check the hash of every file you did not intend to change.\n\n- **The vault copy is the DEPLOY SOURCE — write there first.** The routing\n  layer lives in several places, and `sync-tiiny-router-skill.sh` copies\n  **from** `skills/tiiny-model-routing/` **to** three live skill roots and\n  the plugin directory, because the Hermes install copies are overwritten on\n  every Hermes update and the vault copy is the one that survives. A hand\n  edit to a live copy is therefore a change that **the next deploy erases**.\n  When you add a lesson to this skill, add it to the vault copy first and\n  let the deploy carry it out. Editing the profile copy alone leaves the two\n  divergent in both directions at once: the live copy has the change the\n  vault does not, and the vault has everything else the live copy does not.\n\n- **The deploy script's file filter omits `.pytest_cache`.** It excludes\n  `__pycache__` and `*.pyc`, so a test run that leaves a `.pytest_cache/`\n  directory in `scripts/` gets that directory **copied into every live\n  skill root** on the next deploy. Do not run the deploy script with a\n  dirty `.pytest_cache` present until the filter is widened.\n- **This skill never downloads a model.** It chooses and loads from what is\n  already on the device.\n\n## Delegating coding work\n\nThe discipline for making the appliance do the writing and the manager\nagent do the judging. The full method is in\n[references/coding-delegation.md](references/coding-delegation.md).\n\n### Two rules from the author that decide whether you delegate at all\n\n**A multi-part job delegates; it does not get done inline.** When a task has\nseveral parts — a sweep, a rewrite, a build, a set of independent edits — hand\nthe mechanical parts to a sub-agent on the appliance rather than spending the\npaid model's tokens on them. The appliance has no rate limit and no bill, so a\nlarge job is free there and expensive here. **The manager keeps the diagnosis,\nthe review and the final write** — and \"the final write\" means the edits the\nsub-agent's own verification could not make trustworthy.\n\n**When you are curious about something, ask the author — do not investigate it.**\nCuriosity is a reason to ask a question, not a reason to spend a search. The\nfailure mode is specific: a manager that cannot verify a claim from the\noutside spends many tool calls proving a thing it was already told, and the\nuser watches the token bill grow for a result they could have stated. If the author\nsays he posted it, that **is** the evidence — record it and move on. If you\nneed to know *why* he did something, ask; the answer costs one message and\nteaches the rule you did not have.\n\nThe test for whether a lookup is worth its cost: **will the answer change what\nI write?** If not, it is curiosity — ask instead of digging.\n\n- **The manager does not write the code.** Reaching for `patch` or\n  `write_file` on the target file is the expensive model doing the worker's\n  job, which is the whole thing this discipline exists to prevent. The\n  manager writes the order, reads the diff, runs the acceptance and\n  decides. Exception: a one-edit correction where a delegation round trip\n  would cost more than the fix — make it, and say so plainly.\n- **Make the box match the router, not the router match the box.** To use a\n  specialist, put it on the box `TIINY_BASE_URL` already probes. Do not\n  repoint the endpoint or wait for a restart — `TIINY_BASE_URL` is read at\n  import time, so it only changes in a new process, and a downloaded model\n  costs nothing to load where the router is already looking.\n- **Preflight every dispatch.** `route_check.py --thinking off \"<goal>\"` and\n  read the residency line. If it says NOT loaded, do not force that tier.\n- **Order format.** One job, one file, exact absolute paths, exact anchors\n  (the function, the literal string), a runnable acceptance command, and\n  no `<...>` placeholders — the delegation backend refuses those outright.\n  A 30B appliance needs a tighter brief than a frontier model does.\n- **A child's summary is a self-report.** Run the acceptance command\n  yourself. Correct with a diff and the failure output as evidence, never\n  a re-brief. **Make the acceptance command resolve what the child\n  changed**, not merely assert that a string is present — a path that\n  is present but does not resolve from its file's directory is a broken\n  path wearing a green check. And a rewrite that alters a path *mentioned\n  inside prose* has changed what the sentence says; read the changed\n  lines in context before accepting the batch.\n- **The route label is the invoice.** `tiiny-router` means the box served\n  it; anything else means the paid provider did. Watch it every wave.\n- **One heavy child per box.** Two boxes are two free streams.\n- **Release after every wave.** Stop the specialist, restore the required\n  set.\n\nThe work-order format and the fallback alarm live in\n[references/coding-delegation.md](references/coding-delegation.md);\n[templates/work-order.md](templates/work-order.md) is a starter to copy\nfor each order.\n\n## Verification\n\n\n\n```bash\npython3 scripts/tiiny_ctl.py status                  # device, residency, lock, hold, plan\npython3 scripts/tiiny_ctl.py plan --goal \"your goal\" # tier, model, NPU fit, waves\npython3 scripts/route_check.py --all                 # every tier and its budget\npython3 scripts/route_check.py --thinking off \"goal\" # tier, model, and the reasoning state\npython3 scripts/tiiny_ctl.py catalog --refresh       # what moved in the Model Store\n```\n\nA batch is correct when the plan's model matches the shape of the work, every\nwave fits inside 100% NPU, every wave's models were resident when the child\nran, the child's route label says what you expected, the child's artifact\nsurvives its own claim, and the device was released at the end.\n","numLines":1108,"startLine":1,"totalLines":1108}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_018ffhVSFLaw9wcg9HdYwJpV","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
