{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01UYw4RhX1ugzZMD9zhWp4i4","type":"tool_result","content":"     1\t# The routing backend\n     2\t\n     3\tThe delegation backend that routes each subtask to the model that matches its\n     4\tshape. Install it, then select it in the active profile's config.\n     5\t\n     6\t## What it does\n     7\t\n     8\tCore calls `route_task(task, config)` once per delegated subtask. The\n     9\tbackend inspects that subtask's goal, picks a tier, and returns the model,\n    10\tprovider, endpoint, and request overrides for that subtask alone.\n    11\t\n    12\tWithout it, `delegation.model` pins one model for every child in the batch.\n    13\t\n    14\t**It chooses; it never loads.** The device's resident set is a shared, finite\n    15\tresource and a load is a swap that evicts whatever was resident, so the\n    16\tloading half of the job lives in `scripts/tiiny_ctl.py` — a step the manager\n    17\tagent takes explicitly. See [lifecycle.md](lifecycle.md).\n    18\t\n    19\tThe two halves meet at the **plan** (`~/.hermes/tiiny-plan.json`): the\n    20\tcontroller writes it when it plans a batch, and the router reads it when it\n    21\troutes each subtask. That is how a specialist the controller just loaded\n    22\tbecomes the specialist the child actually runs on.\n    23\t\n    24\t## Install\n    25\t\n    26\t1. Copy the plugin directory into your Hermes plugins tree. **All three files\n    27\t   matter** — `tiers.py` holds the table the plugin and the scripts share, and\n    28\t   `thinking.py` holds the reasoning-mode control:\n    29\t\n    30\t   ```bash\n    31\t   cp -r plugin ~/.hermes/hermes-agent/plugins/delegation/tiiny-router/\n    32\t   ```\n    33\t\n    34\t2. Add the device as a provider in the active profile's `config.yaml`.\n    35\t   `hermes-tiiny-router install --configure` does this and the backend\n    36\t   selection in step 3 through `hermes config set` (everything except\n    37\t   `fallback_providers`, which depends on your paid provider):\n    38\t\n    39\t   ```yaml\n    40\t   providers:\n    41\t     tiiny:\n    42\t       name: tiiny\n    43\t       api: http://openai.api.tiiny/v1\n    44\t       key_env: TIINY_API_KEY\n    45\t       transport: chat_completions\n    46\t       default_model: Qwen/Qwen3.6-35B-A3B\n    47\t       context_length: 262144\n    48\t       models_discovered: false\n    49\t       extra_body:\n    50\t         chat_template_kwargs:\n    51\t           enable_thinking: false\n    52\t       models:\n    53\t         Qwen/Qwen3.6-35B-A3B: {context_length: 262144}\n    54\t         Qwen/Qwen3.8-27B: {context_length: 262144}\n    55\t         Qwen/Qwen3.5-9B: {context_length: 131072}\n    56\t         openai/gpt-oss-20b: {context_length: 131072}\n    57\t         ornith-ai/Ornith-1.5-35B-A3B-Turbo: {context_length: 262144}\n    58\t         InternScience/Sage-A1.5-Turbo: {context_length: 262144}\n    59\t   ```\n    60\t\n    61\t   The bearer goes in the environment as `TIINY_API_KEY`.\n    62\t\n    63\t   `models_discovered: false` matters: with it on, a live probe can wipe the\n    64\t   metadata above and leave a model with the wrong context length.\n    65\t\n    66\t3. Select the backend in the same `config.yaml`:\n    67\t\n    68\t   ```yaml\n    69\t   delegation:\n    70\t     backend: tiiny-router\n    71\t     fallback_providers:\n    72\t       - provider: minimax\n    73\t         model: MiniMax-M3\n    74\t   ```\n    75\t\n    76\t   The fallback matters: if the device is unreachable, the batch should still\n    77\t   complete on a paid provider rather than fail. It should also be visible\n    78\t   when it happens — a silent fallback means the local tier is not actually\n    79\t   being used.\n    80\t\n    81\t4. Restart the agent so the plugin and the new schema are picked up. If the\n    82\t   agent runs under a gateway that cannot be restarted, the gateway's\n    83\t   control socket reloads plugins in place:\n    84\t\n    85\t   ```python\n    86\t   from pathlib import Path\n    87\t   from gateway.control_socket import reload_gateway_plugins\n    88\t   from hermes_constants import get_hermes_home\n    89\t\n    90\t   reload_gateway_plugins(Path(get_hermes_home()))\n    91\t   ```\n    92\t\n    93\t## Verify\n    94\t\n    95\t```bash\n    96\tpython3 ../scripts/route_check.py \"index every file under the vault and classify each\"\n    97\tpython3 ../scripts/route_check.py --thinking off \"index every file under the vault\"\n    98\tpython3 ../scripts/tiiny_ctl.py status\n    99\t```\n   100\t\n   101\tThe checker prints the tier, the model, that model's NPU share and context\n   102\twindow, what the model can do with reasoning, and whether it is currently\n   103\tresident. The controller prints the device, the resident set, the lock, the\n   104\thold and the plan.\n   105\t\n   106\tTo confirm the backend is live, delegate a batch with mixed subtasks and read\n   107\tthe `route` on each child's result — it carries the backend name and the\n   108\ttier label, with the achieved reasoning state on it.\n   109\t\n   110\t## Thinking mode\n   160\t**Re-read these numbers before trusting them.** They came from one device's\n   161\tcatalogue endpoint on 2026-11-09, when the store held 92 models. The store\n   162\tchanges with every TiinyOS update, and the NPU shares have already moved\n   163\tonce.\n   164\t\n   165\tThe reasoning column is the same kind of snapshot, and the device is the\n   166\tauthority on it: the router reads the capability at route time, so a stale\n   167\trow here cannot break a route.\n   168\t\n   169\tThey live in **`plugin/tiers.py`**, which is the single source of truth: the\n   170\tplugin, the controller and the checker all read that one file, so they cannot\n   171\tdrift apart. It is loaded by path rather than by package name, because the\n   172\tHermes plugin loader executes `__init__.py` through `spec_from_file_location`\n   173\t— which does not establish package semantics, so a relative import would\n   174\tfail there. `plugin/thinking.py` is loaded the same way, and is kept free of\n   175\tHermes imports so the standalone checker can use it too.\n   176\t\n   177\t## Changing a tier\n   178\t\n   179\tThree edits, in this order, all in `plugin/tiers.py`:\n   180\t\n   181\t1. `TIER_CHOICES` — the model id, its NPU share, and its context window. All\n   182\t   three come from the device; `tiiny_ctl.py catalog --refresh` tells you\n   183\t   when they have moved.\n   184\t2. `TIER_MODEL` — derived from `TIER_CHOICES`; it needs no separate edit\n   185\t   unless a tier's default changes.\n   186\t3. `SHAPE_SIGNALS` — the signals that route a task to that tier. More\n   187\t   specific shapes come first; the first tier with a matching signal wins.\n   188\t\n   189\tThen update the same table in `SKILL.md`, and re-run the checker on a known\n   190\tgoal.\n   191\t\n   192\t## Things that will bite\n   193\t\n   194\t- **The route's shape is the credential path.** The backend returns the\n   195\t  delegation section's own shape with only the model overridden — the way\n   196\t  the default backend returns `dict(config)`. This is not cosmetic. A route\n   197\t  with `base_url` set and `api_key` empty is a *direct endpoint*, and an\n   198\t  empty key means **inherit the parent's key**. The parent runs on the paid\n   199\t  provider, so the device would receive the wrong bearer and answer\n   200\t  `401 auth_failed` — and the child would fall back to the paid provider,\n   201\t  invisibly. Returning the section's shape puts this backend on the\n   202\t  provider-resolution branch, where the device's `key_env` is honoured.\n   203\t- **The router reads residency, and that changes the route.** It queries\n   204\t  `GET /v1/models` on the inference endpoint (30-second cache) and picks a\n   205\t  model that is actually loaded: the plan's assignment if there is one, then\n   206\t  the tier's first choice, then another candidate from the same tier, then\n   207\t  the generalist. A model that is not resident answers `404 \"is not loaded\"`,\n   208\t  which Hermes treats as terminal and falls back to the paid provider — so\n   209\t  routing to an unloaded model silently moves work off the device. The\n   210\t  route's `label` records any substitution (`code->generalist`). If the\n   211\t  lookup fails, the router fails open to the tier's first choice.\n   212\t- **The router never loads a model.** A load is a swap, and it evicts\n   213\t  whatever was resident — including the model a sibling task is using. The\n   214\t  controller does the loading, explicitly, under a lock.\n   215\t- **An unknown tier is refused.** The override fails loudly rather than\n   216\t  falling back to the generalist, because a silent fallback is invisible in\n   217\t  the output and expensive in the tokens.\n   218\t- **The task schema is cached.** `task_parameters()` and `description()` must\n   219\t  be deterministic for a given config — the schema is part of the cached\n   220\t  prompt prefix, so a non-deterministic result changes the prompt between\n   221\t  calls.\n   222\t- **Thinking mode is per task, not per config.** `thinking: \"auto\" | \"on\" |\n   223\t  \"off\"` is a task parameter, and the route's label reports the state the\n   224\t  model actually reached. Do not read \"off\" in the request as \"off\" in the\n   225\t  model: a model with no switch ends up at its cheapest level instead, and\n   226\t  the label says `off→low`.\n   227\t- **Two reasoning knobs, and they go in different places.**\n   228\t  `enable_thinking` belongs in `extra_body.chat_template_kwargs`; a named\n   229\t  level belongs at the **top level of the request**, not inside\n   230\t  `chat_template_kwargs`. The same level name inside the chat template is a\n   231\t  different knob that some model families read and others ignore silently —\n   232\t  which looks configured and changes nothing.\n   233\t- **The device's `toggleable` flag has been wrong.** A model flagged as\n   234\t  having no on/off switch honours it anyway. Send the boolean whenever it\n   235\t  can be sent, and let the label report what actually took effect.\n   236\t- **A reasoning model with a small `max_tokens` returns an empty response**\n   237\t  with the budget spent in the trace, and the status code is 200. The router\n   238\t  applies an output floor for this reason.\n   239\t- **Hermes secrets are not in the environment.** The device key lives in\n   240\t  `~/.hermes/.env` and is read through `hermes_cli.config`'s accessor. A\n   241\t  plugin that reads `os.environ` finds nothing, and the residency check\n   242\t  fails open on every call.\n   243\t- **A partially-installed plugin fails at load, not at use.** The plugin\n   244\t  loader executes `__init__.py` through `spec_from_file_location`, so\n   245\t  `__init__.py` cannot use a relative import to reach `tiers.py` — it loads\n   246\t  that file by path. Copy the whole `plugin/` directory, not just\n   247\t  `__init__.py` and `plugin.yaml`. A tree with `__init__.py` but no\n   248\t  `tiers.py` raises `ImportError` at load, and the backend is then simply\n   249\t  absent from `hermes plugins list` — which reads as \"never installed\"\n   250\t  rather than \"installed wrong\". Validate with\n   251\t  `hermes plugins validate <dir>`. The same applies to `thinking.py`.\n   252\t- **The installed skill and the vault skill drift apart.** The vault copy\n   253\t  under `skills/tiiny-model-routing/` is the source of truth; the installed\n   254\t  copies live under `~/.hermes/skills/tiiny-model-routing/` and\n   255\t  `~/.hermes/skills/mlops/tiiny-model-routing/`. A stale installed copy ends\n   256\t  up carrying its own literal copy of the tier table instead of importing\n   257\t  `plugin/tiers.py`, and the table starts drifting. Diff all three before\n   258\t  trusting any of them, and re-sync vault → installed after any table change.\n   259\t- **A saved `delegation.backend` does not change a running chat.** The\n   260\t  `delegate_task` schema is cached in the prompt prefix, so a config edit\n   261\t  applies from the next session (`/new`), not the current one. Verify the\n   262\t  backend in a fresh process rather than expecting the current session to\n   263\t  re-route:\n   264\t  `python3 -c \"from agent import delegation_backends as db; print(db.list_backends())\"`","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"95803e3d-040d-4401-b98c-4ca7b716b706","uuid":"f55f6a7e-07f7-44a6-8783-7939844d28d9","timestamp":"2026-11-21T05:59:11.836Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"     1\t# The routing backend\n     2\t\n     3\tThe delegation backend that routes each subtask to the model that matches its\n     4\tshape. Install it, then select it in the active profile's config.\n     5\t\n     6\t## What it does\n     7\t\n     8\tCore calls `route_task(task, config)` once per delegated subtask. The\n     9\tbackend inspects that subtask's goal, picks a tier, and returns the model,\n    10\tprovider, endpoint, and request overrides for that subtask alone.\n    11\t\n    12\tWithout it, `delegation.model` pins one model for every child in the batch.\n    13\t\n    14\t**It chooses; it never loads.** The device's resident set is a shared, finite\n    15\tresource and a load is a swap that evicts whatever was resident, so the\n    16\tloading half of the job lives in `scripts/tiiny_ctl.py` — a step the manager\n    17\tagent takes explicitly. See [lifecycle.md](lifecycle.md).\n    18\t\n    19\tThe two halves meet at the **plan** (`~/.hermes/tiiny-plan.json`): the\n    20\tcontroller writes it when it plans a batch, and the router reads it when it\n    21\troutes each subtask. That is how a specialist the controller just loaded\n    22\tbecomes the specialist the child actually runs on.\n    23\t\n    24\t## Install\n    25\t\n    26\t1. Copy the plugin directory into your Hermes plugins tree. **All three files\n    27\t   matter** — `tiers.py` holds the table the plugin and the scripts share, and\n    28\t   `thinking.py` holds the reasoning-mode control:\n    29\t\n    30\t   ```bash\n    31\t   cp -r plugin ~/.hermes/hermes-agent/plugins/delegation/tiiny-router/\n    32\t   ```\n    33\t\n    34\t2. Add the device as a provider in the active profile's `config.yaml`.\n    35\t   `hermes-tiiny-router install --configure` does this and the backend\n    36\t   selection in step 3 through `hermes config set` (everything except\n    37\t   `fallback_providers`, which depends on your paid provider):\n    38\t\n    39\t   ```yaml\n    40\t   providers:\n    41\t     tiiny:\n    42\t       name: tiiny\n    43\t       api: http://openai.api.tiiny/v1\n    44\t       key_env: TIINY_API_KEY\n    45\t       transport: chat_completions\n    46\t       default_model: Qwen/Qwen3.6-35B-A3B\n    47\t       context_length: 262144\n    48\t       models_discovered: false\n    49\t       extra_body:\n    50\t         chat_template_kwargs:\n    51\t           enable_thinking: false\n    52\t       models:\n    53\t         Qwen/Qwen3.6-35B-A3B: {context_length: 262144}\n    54\t         Qwen/Qwen3.8-27B: {context_length: 262144}\n    55\t         Qwen/Qwen3.5-9B: {context_length: 131072}\n    56\t         openai/gpt-oss-20b: {context_length: 131072}\n    57\t         ornith-ai/Ornith-1.5-35B-A3B-Turbo: {context_length: 262144}\n    58\t         InternScience/Sage-A1.5-Turbo: {context_length: 262144}\n    59\t   ```\n    60\t\n    61\t   The bearer goes in the environment as `TIINY_API_KEY`.\n    62\t\n    63\t   `models_discovered: false` matters: with it on, a live probe can wipe the\n    64\t   metadata above and leave a model with the wrong context length.\n    65\t\n    66\t3. Select the backend in the same `config.yaml`:\n    67\t\n    68\t   ```yaml\n    69\t   delegation:\n    70\t     backend: tiiny-router\n    71\t     fallback_providers:\n    72\t       - provider: minimax\n    73\t         model: MiniMax-M3\n    74\t   ```\n    75\t\n    76\t   The fallback matters: if the device is unreachable, the batch should still\n    77\t   complete on a paid provider rather than fail. It should also be visible\n    78\t   when it happens — a silent fallback means the local tier is not actually\n    79\t   being used.\n    80\t\n    81\t4. Restart the agent so the plugin and the new schema are picked up. If the\n    82\t   agent runs under a gateway that cannot be restarted, the gateway's\n    83\t   control socket reloads plugins in place:\n    84\t\n    85\t   ```python\n    86\t   from pathlib import Path\n    87\t   from gateway.control_socket import reload_gateway_plugins\n    88\t   from hermes_constants import get_hermes_home\n    89\t\n    90\t   reload_gateway_plugins(Path(get_hermes_home()))\n    91\t   ```\n    92\t\n    93\t## Verify\n    94\t\n    95\t```bash\n    96\tpython3 ../scripts/route_check.py \"index every file under the vault and classify each\"\n    97\tpython3 ../scripts/route_check.py --thinking off \"index every file under the vault\"\n    98\tpython3 ../scripts/tiiny_ctl.py status\n    99\t```\n   100\t\n   101\tThe checker prints the tier, the model, that model's NPU share and context\n   102\twindow, what the model can do with reasoning, and whether it is currently\n   103\tresident. The controller prints the device, the resident set, the lock, the\n   104\thold and the plan.\n   105\t\n   106\tTo confirm the backend is live, delegate a batch with mixed subtasks and read\n   107\tthe `route` on each child's result — it carries the backend name and the\n   108\ttier label, with the achieved reasoning state on it.\n   109\t\n   110\t## Thinking mode\n   160\t**Re-read these numbers before trusting them.** They came from one device's\n   161\tcatalogue endpoint on 2026-11-09, when the store held 92 models. The store\n   162\tchanges with every TiinyOS update, and the NPU shares have already moved\n   163\tonce.\n   164\t\n   165\tThe reasoning column is the same kind of snapshot, and the device is the\n   166\tauthority on it: the router reads the capability at route time, so a stale\n   167\trow here cannot break a route.\n   168\t\n   169\tThey live in **`plugin/tiers.py`**, which is the single source of truth: the\n   170\tplugin, the controller and the checker all read that one file, so they cannot\n   171\tdrift apart. It is loaded by path rather than by package name, because the\n   172\tHermes plugin loader executes `__init__.py` through `spec_from_file_location`\n   173\t— which does not establish package semantics, so a relative import would\n   174\tfail there. `plugin/thinking.py` is loaded the same way, and is kept free of\n   175\tHermes imports so the standalone checker can use it too.\n   176\t\n   177\t## Changing a tier\n   178\t\n   179\tThree edits, in this order, all in `plugin/tiers.py`:\n   180\t\n   181\t1. `TIER_CHOICES` — the model id, its NPU share, and its context window. All\n   182\t   three come from the device; `tiiny_ctl.py catalog --refresh` tells you\n   183\t   when they have moved.\n   184\t2. `TIER_MODEL` — derived from `TIER_CHOICES`; it needs no separate edit\n   185\t   unless a tier's default changes.\n   186\t3. `SHAPE_SIGNALS` — the signals that route a task to that tier. More\n   187\t   specific shapes come first; the first tier with a matching signal wins.\n   188\t\n   189\tThen update the same table in `SKILL.md`, and re-run the checker on a known\n   190\tgoal.\n   191\t\n   192\t## Things that will bite\n   193\t\n   194\t- **The route's shape is the credential path.** The backend returns the\n   195\t  delegation section's own shape with only the model overridden — the way\n   196\t  the default backend returns `dict(config)`. This is not cosmetic. A route\n   197\t  with `base_url` set and `api_key` empty is a *direct endpoint*, and an\n   198\t  empty key means **inherit the parent's key**. The parent runs on the paid\n   199\t  provider, so the device would receive the wrong bearer and answer\n   200\t  `401 auth_failed` — and the child would fall back to the paid provider,\n   201\t  invisibly. Returning the section's shape puts this backend on the\n   202\t  provider-resolution branch, where the device's `key_env` is honoured.\n   203\t- **The router reads residency, and that changes the route.** It queries\n   204\t  `GET /v1/models` on the inference endpoint (30-second cache) and picks a\n   205\t  model that is actually loaded: the plan's assignment if there is one, then\n   206\t  the tier's first choice, then another candidate from the same tier, then\n   207\t  the generalist. A model that is not resident answers `404 \"is not loaded\"`,\n   208\t  which Hermes treats as terminal and falls back to the paid provider — so\n   209\t  routing to an unloaded model silently moves work off the device. The\n   210\t  route's `label` records any substitution (`code->generalist`). If the\n   211\t  lookup fails, the router fails open to the tier's first choice.\n   212\t- **The router never loads a model.** A load is a swap, and it evicts\n   213\t  whatever was resident — including the model a sibling task is using. The\n   214\t  controller does the loading, explicitly, under a lock.\n   215\t- **An unknown tier is refused.** The override fails loudly rather than\n   216\t  falling back to the generalist, because a silent fallback is invisible in\n   217\t  the output and expensive in the tokens.\n   218\t- **The task schema is cached.** `task_parameters()` and `description()` must\n   219\t  be deterministic for a given config — the schema is part of the cached\n   220\t  prompt prefix, so a non-deterministic result changes the prompt between\n   221\t  calls.\n   222\t- **Thinking mode is per task, not per config.** `thinking: \"auto\" | \"on\" |\n   223\t  \"off\"` is a task parameter, and the route's label reports the state the\n   224\t  model actually reached. Do not read \"off\" in the request as \"off\" in the\n   225\t  model: a model with no switch ends up at its cheapest level instead, and\n   226\t  the label says `off→low`.\n   227\t- **Two reasoning knobs, and they go in different places.**\n   228\t  `enable_thinking` belongs in `extra_body.chat_template_kwargs`; a named\n   229\t  level belongs at the **top level of the request**, not inside\n   230\t  `chat_template_kwargs`. The same level name inside the chat template is a\n   231\t  different knob that some model families read and others ignore silently —\n   232\t  which looks configured and changes nothing.\n   233\t- **The device's `toggleable` flag has been wrong.** A model flagged as\n   234\t  having no on/off switch honours it anyway. Send the boolean whenever it\n   235\t  can be sent, and let the label report what actually took effect.\n   236\t- **A reasoning model with a small `max_tokens` returns an empty response**\n   237\t  with the budget spent in the trace, and the status code is 200. The router\n   238\t  applies an output floor for this reason.\n   239\t- **Hermes secrets are not in the environment.** The device key lives in\n   240\t  `~/.hermes/.env` and is read through `hermes_cli.config`'s accessor. A\n   241\t  plugin that reads `os.environ` finds nothing, and the residency check\n   242\t  fails open on every call.\n   243\t- **A partially-installed plugin fails at load, not at use.** The plugin\n   244\t  loader executes `__init__.py` through `spec_from_file_location`, so\n   245\t  `__init__.py` cannot use a relative import to reach `tiers.py` — it loads\n   246\t  that file by path. Copy the whole `plugin/` directory, not just\n   247\t  `__init__.py` and `plugin.yaml`. A tree with `__init__.py` but no\n   248\t  `tiers.py` raises `ImportError` at load, and the backend is then simply\n   249\t  absent from `hermes plugins list` — which reads as \"never installed\"\n   250\t  rather than \"installed wrong\". Validate with\n   251\t  `hermes plugins validate <dir>`. The same applies to `thinking.py`.\n   252\t- **The installed skill and the vault skill drift apart.** The vault copy\n   253\t  under `skills/tiiny-model-routing/` is the source of truth; the installed\n   254\t  copies live under `~/.hermes/skills/tiiny-model-routing/` and\n   255\t  `~/.hermes/skills/mlops/tiiny-model-routing/`. A stale installed copy ends\n   256\t  up carrying its own literal copy of the tier table instead of importing\n   257\t  `plugin/tiers.py`, and the table starts drifting. Diff all three before\n   258\t  trusting any of them, and re-sync vault → installed after any table change.\n   259\t- **A saved `delegation.backend` does not change a running chat.** The\n   260\t  `delegate_task` schema is cached in the prompt prefix, so a config edit\n   261\t  applies from the next session (`/new`), not the current one. Verify the\n   262\t  backend in a fresh process rather than expecting the current session to\n   263\t  re-route:\n   264\t  `python3 -c \"from agent import delegation_backends as db; print(db.list_backends())\"`","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01UYw4RhX1ugzZMD9zhWp4i4","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
