{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01NgCyCj7QehaHmZKjBaMhFu","type":"tool_result","content":"    25\tThe plan is shared with the router, which is why it lives in the **base**\n    26\tHermes home: every profile's manager agent must see the same plan.\n    27\t\n    28\t## The lock\n    29\t\n    30\t### The mutex\n    31\t\n    32\t`~/.hermes/tiiny-lock.json`. Its contents are the lock:\n    33\t\n    34\t```json\n    35\t{\n    36\t  \"state\": \"held\",\n    37\t  \"owner\": \"<agent>\",\n    38\t  \"task\": \"vault index + research sweep\",\n    39\t  \"models\": [\"openai/gpt-oss-20b\"],\n    40\t  \"created\": 1793564901.2,\n    41\t  \"expires\": 1793566701.2,\n    42\t  \"pid\": 4711\n    43\t}\n    44\t```\n    45\t\n    46\tIt is taken under `flock` on the file, so two agents racing for it are\n    47\tserialised by the kernel rather than by hope. The critical section is short —\n    48\tread, check, write — and the lock itself outlives the process, because the\n    49\tfile's contents are the lock.\n    50\t\n    51\t**A lock that expires is not a lock that can wedge the device.** The\n    52\texpiry is what makes a crashed agent survivable: the next agent to reserve\n    53\ttakes over an expired lock and says so, rather than being told the device\n    54\thas been busy for six hours. A lock with no expiry would need a human to\n    55\tclear it, and a human is exactly what is missing when a batch fails at 3am.\n    56\t\n    57\t**Release is a write, not a delete.** The sandbox this runs in cannot unlink\n    58\tfiles outside the session workspace, so the released state is written into\n    59\tthe file and the file stays. An empty or released lock file is a released\n    60\tlock.\n    61\t\n    62\t### The announcement\n    63\t\n    64\tThe mutex is machine truth, and only Hermes profiles read it. The AMC's\n    65\tshared surface is `hot.md` — the vault-wide board of what is in flight — so\n    66\tthe lock is announced there in a marked block:\n    67\t\n    68\t```markdown\n    69\t<!-- tiiny-device-lock:start -->\n    70\t**Tiiny is BUSY — do not schedule device work.**\n    71\tHeld by `<agent>` for vault index + research sweep since 12:41. Models:\n    72\topenai/gpt-oss-20b. Lock expires 13:11.\n    73\t<!-- tiiny-device-lock:end -->\n    74\t```\n    75\t\n    76\tThe markers make the edit idempotent and contained: the controller replaces\n    77\tthe text between them and touches nothing else in the file, so an agent\n    78\tediting `hot.md` elsewhere is not disturbed. If the markers are absent, the\n    79\tblock is appended.\n    80\t\n    81\t## The hold\n    82\t\n    83\t`~/.hermes/tiiny-hold` records what an agent deliberately loaded: *these\n    84\tmodels are resident because this batch asked for them.*\n    85\t\n    86\t**It used to be an interlock.** A five-minute watchdog read this file and\n    87\treloaded nothing while it existed, so the hold was what stopped a policy\n    88\tpoller from evicting a long batch's model mid-run. That watchdog was retired\n    89\ton 2026-11-10, so the file no longer stops anything from firing.\n    90\t\n    91\tWhat replaced it is `tiiny_ctl.py ensure --model <id>`, called before\n    92\tdelegating. It is idempotent, it checks the cross-agent lock, and it fails\n    93\tloudly when a model does not land — which is the property the hold could\n    94\tnever provide, because a hold only ever *prevented* something.\n    95\t\n    96\tThree rules still hold, because they are about the file's shape:\n    97\t\n    98\t1. **Write it before the load.** `status` should report what the batch is\n    99\t   about to have, not what it had a moment ago.\n   100\t2. **The first line is the one that matters.** In a multi-wave batch the\n    55\t   constraint and choosing on window size alone picks the wrong model.\n    56\t   Context decides only when the repo or transcript actually approaches it.\n    57\t\n    58\t## Topology on two boxes\n    59\t\n    60\tTwo boxes are two independent generation streams, and each is still\n    61\tsingle-stream internally. The shape that uses both:\n    62\t\n    63\t1. **Plan phase** on the flex box (the one that does not declare\n    64\t   `keep_resident` for the generalist) — it is expected to be swapped.\n    65\t2. **Run phase**: the manager stays on the box that already holds the\n    66\t   generalist, so it needs no swap and survives reboots if restore is enabled;\n    67\t   workers go to the flex box.\n    68\t\n    69\tTwo consequences worth stating out loud before the run:\n    70\t\n    71\t- The plan phase and the workers share the flex box, so they are **sequential**.\n    72\t  Finish planning before loading a worker model, or the worker load evicts the\n    73\t  planner mid-thought.\n    74\t- Loading a worker on the flex box evicts that box's resident planner; a\n    75\t  manager that retries a failed turn onto the flex box evicts it too. See the\n    76\t  mesh-policy pitfall in SKILL.md.\n    77\t\n    78\t## Reporting it to the user\n    79\t\n    80\tGive the pick, the box, the NPU share and the reasoning setting in a table,\n    81\tthen one line each on *why that model* and *what to avoid*. Name the\n    82\tsolo-occupant and the dormant-orchestration cases explicitly — they are the\n    83\tchoices a reader would otherwise make confidently and wrongly.\n     1\t# Two-box capability difference matrix (read-only, verified 2026-11-13)\n     2\t\n     3\tProcedure for answering \"which box do I ask for this?\" across the two Tiiny\n     4\tappliances. Produces a per-model capability classification and a per-box\n     5\tsummary from **live device metadata only**.\n     6\t\n     7\t## The two boxes\n     8\t\n     9\t| | TinyAnt (vault) | TiinyOcto (flex) |\n    10\t|---|---|---|\n    11\t| IP | 198.51.100.198 | 198.51.100.192 |\n    12\t| Device ID | TNYM…redacted | TNYM…redacted |\n    13\t| Key env var | `TIINY_API_KEY` | `TIINY_API_KEY_2` |\n    14\t\n    15\tBoth carry the **same 93-model store** — identical model ids, and identical\n    16\t`npu_usage` / `context_window` / `type` / `capabilities` / `params` for every\n    17\tone of the 93. They differ **only in download state and residency**. Verified\n    18\tacross all 93 ids on 2026-11-13.\n    19\t\n    20\t## The endpoints that matter\n    21\t\n    22\t| Endpoint | Returns | Notes |\n    23\t|---|---|---|\n    24\t| `GET /api/v1/models/online_models` | full 93-model catalogue | **The store truth.** Carries `status`, `progress`, `npu_usage`, `context_window`, `type`, `capabilities`, `supported`, `input`, `output`, `params`, `desc`, `thinking`, `reasoning_levels` |\n    25\t| `GET /api/v1/models/running` | running + pending + instances | **The residency truth.** Each running instance carries its own `npu_usage` |\n    26\t| `GET /api/v1/npu/status` | one device: `model`, `mem_total_mb`, `mem_used_mb` | The **only** endpoint that gives the NPU budget |\n    27\t| `GET /api/v1/sys/status` -> `npus[]` | live working set | **Not the budget** — see below |\n    28\t| `GET /v1/models` | resident models only | Alias `default`; matches the store's `running` entries |\n    29\t\n    30\t`/v1/models` on the box lists only **resident** models. `/api/v1/models` lists\n   125\tSo the generalist never requires choosing a box; the choice only matters for\n   126\tspecialists, and TiinyOcto covers every category TinyAnt does.\n   127\t\n   128\t## Driving both boxes at once\n   129\t\n   130\tThe two boxes are separate hosts with separate NPUs, so **both can be managed\n   131\tconcurrently** — but only over HTTP. The paths differ and the difference costs\n   132\tan afternoon if it is discovered mid-sprint.\n   133\t\n   134\t| Path | Both boxes at once? | Why |\n   135\t|---|---|---|\n   136\t| `POST /mesh/lifecycle` `{action, model, target}` on the mesh gateway | **yes** | one call per target, gateway key, reaches each box over its own bearer, needs no CLI session |\n   137\t| `POST /api/v1/models/<id>/start\\|stop` on the box, with that box's bearer | **yes** | what the lifecycle route does underneath; no session needed |\n   138\t| `tiiny_ctl.py load/unload` | **no** | `device_key()` reads only `TIINY_API_KEY`, `MANAGEMENT_BASE` only `TIINY_HOST`, and both shell out to `tiiny load`, whose session is bound to one device |\n   139\t| `tiiny load` by hand | **no** | signing in for the second box signs the first out |\n   140\t\n   141\t**Driving the second box with the controller** — per-box env in that shell\n   142\tonly; the four state files matter, or the two controllers contend for one lock:\n   143\t\n   144\t```bash\n   145\tTIINY_HOST=http://198.51.100.192 \\\n   146\tTIINY_API_KEY=\"$TIINY_API_KEY_2\" \\\n   147\tTIINY_LOCK_FILE=~/.hermes/tiiny-octo-lock.json \\\n   148\tTIINY_PLAN_FILE=~/.hermes/tiiny-octo-plan.json \\\n   149\tTIINY_HOLD_FILE=~/.hermes/tiiny-octo-hold \\\n   150\tTIINY_SNAPSHOT_FILE=~/.hermes/tiiny-octo-snapshot.json \\\n   151\t  python3 scripts/tiiny_ctl.py status\n   152\t```\n   153\t\n   154\t**Concurrent load, verified** — start the same model on both boxes from two\n   155\tthreads, then read residency back from each device:\n   156\t\n   157\t```python\n   158\timport json, threading, urllib.request\n   159\tkey = open(os.path.expanduser(\"~/.hermes/mesh/gateway_key\")).read().strip()\n   160\tdef post(target):\n   161\t    body = json.dumps({\"action\": \"start\", \"model\": MODEL, \"target\": target}).encode()\n   162\t    r = urllib.request.Request(\"http://127.0.0.1:8767/mesh/lifecycle\", data=body,\n   163\t        headers={\"Authorization\": f\"Bearer {key}\", \"Content-Type\": \"application/json\"},\n   164\t        method=\"POST\")\n   165\t    urllib.request.urlopen(r, timeout=120)\n   166\t```\n   167\t\n   168\tBoth answers are `ok` and both models land; the incumbents survive because the\n   169\trequested model fit the free share. Undo with the same call and `action: stop`\n   170\t— always leave the residency you found.\n   171\t\n   172\t**The two boxes do not agree on error shape, so never judge by the response\n   173\tcode.** Stopping a model that was not resident returned HTTP 500\n   174\t`Internal Server Error` on one box and `ok: true` with\n   175\t`stop_reason: \"explicit_stop\"` on the other, for the same request. Read\n   176\t`GET /api/v1/models/running` on the box and treat a 500 from a non-resident\n   177\tstop as \"nothing to stop\", not a failed unload.\n   178\t\n   179\t**Two boxes means two waves in flight, not two children per box.** Each box is\n   180\tstill single-stream, so a four-wave plan runs as two concurrent lanes of two\n   181\tsequential waves.\n   182\t\n   183\t## Device-side data bugs found\n   184\t\n   185\t1. `TaoBaiBai2025/Z-Image-Turbo-Creating-Realistic` carries the\n   186\t   **CharacterDesignSheet** description verbatim — the catalogue's own\n   187\t   copy-paste error, and it picks the wrong image model if you trust the desc.\n   188\t2. `Qwen/Qwen-Image-2.1-PE-T2I` is typed `Text Generation` but is really an\n   189\t   image prompt expander (`supported: ['Prompt Enhance']`).\n   190\t3. Two Octo-ready models carry `need_update` with no `update_title`:\n   191\t   `Qwen/Qwen-Image-2.1-PE-T2I`, `openai/Whisper-large-v3-turbo`.\n   192\t\n   193\t## Reproducible script\n   194\t\n   195\t`scripts/tiiny_two_box_matrix.py` (in this skill directory) runs the whole\n   196\tread-only pass: fetches both catalogues, classifies every model with the\n   197\tevidence recorded, writes the markdown matrix, and re-reads both devices to\n   198\tverify every headline number before exiting non-zero on any mismatch.\n   199\t\n   200\t```bash\n   201\tpython3 scripts/tiiny_two_box_matrix.py            # writes the matrix\n   202\tpython3 scripts/tiiny_two_box_matrix.py --verify   # verify an existing matrix\n   203\t```\n   204\t\n   205\tIt never loads, unloads, starts, stops or downloads anything. It reads\n   206\t`~/.hermes/.env` for the two key env vars by name and never prints a value.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"95803e3d-040d-4401-b98c-4ca7b716b706","uuid":"45de62dc-6b47-43d7-b355-65bab8b84948","timestamp":"2026-11-21T05:59:37.783Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"    25\tThe plan is shared with the router, which is why it lives in the **base**\n    26\tHermes home: every profile's manager agent must see the same plan.\n    27\t\n    28\t## The lock\n    29\t\n    30\t### The mutex\n    31\t\n    32\t`~/.hermes/tiiny-lock.json`. Its contents are the lock:\n    33\t\n    34\t```json\n    35\t{\n    36\t  \"state\": \"held\",\n    37\t  \"owner\": \"<agent>\",\n    38\t  \"task\": \"vault index + research sweep\",\n    39\t  \"models\": [\"openai/gpt-oss-20b\"],\n    40\t  \"created\": 1793564901.2,\n    41\t  \"expires\": 1793566701.2,\n    42\t  \"pid\": 4711\n    43\t}\n    44\t```\n    45\t\n    46\tIt is taken under `flock` on the file, so two agents racing for it are\n    47\tserialised by the kernel rather than by hope. The critical section is short —\n    48\tread, check, write — and the lock itself outlives the process, because the\n    49\tfile's contents are the lock.\n    50\t\n    51\t**A lock that expires is not a lock that can wedge the device.** The\n    52\texpiry is what makes a crashed agent survivable: the next agent to reserve\n    53\ttakes over an expired lock and says so, rather than being told the device\n    54\thas been busy for six hours. A lock with no expiry would need a human to\n    55\tclear it, and a human is exactly what is missing when a batch fails at 3am.\n    56\t\n    57\t**Release is a write, not a delete.** The sandbox this runs in cannot unlink\n    58\tfiles outside the session workspace, so the released state is written into\n    59\tthe file and the file stays. An empty or released lock file is a released\n    60\tlock.\n    61\t\n    62\t### The announcement\n    63\t\n    64\tThe mutex is machine truth, and only Hermes profiles read it. The AMC's\n    65\tshared surface is `hot.md` — the vault-wide board of what is in flight — so\n    66\tthe lock is announced there in a marked block:\n    67\t\n    68\t```markdown\n    69\t<!-- tiiny-device-lock:start -->\n    70\t**Tiiny is BUSY — do not schedule device work.**\n    71\tHeld by `<agent>` for vault index + research sweep since 12:41. Models:\n    72\topenai/gpt-oss-20b. Lock expires 13:11.\n    73\t<!-- tiiny-device-lock:end -->\n    74\t```\n    75\t\n    76\tThe markers make the edit idempotent and contained: the controller replaces\n    77\tthe text between them and touches nothing else in the file, so an agent\n    78\tediting `hot.md` elsewhere is not disturbed. If the markers are absent, the\n    79\tblock is appended.\n    80\t\n    81\t## The hold\n    82\t\n    83\t`~/.hermes/tiiny-hold` records what an agent deliberately loaded: *these\n    84\tmodels are resident because this batch asked for them.*\n    85\t\n    86\t**It used to be an interlock.** A five-minute watchdog read this file and\n    87\treloaded nothing while it existed, so the hold was what stopped a policy\n    88\tpoller from evicting a long batch's model mid-run. That watchdog was retired\n    89\ton 2026-11-10, so the file no longer stops anything from firing.\n    90\t\n    91\tWhat replaced it is `tiiny_ctl.py ensure --model <id>`, called before\n    92\tdelegating. It is idempotent, it checks the cross-agent lock, and it fails\n    93\tloudly when a model does not land — which is the property the hold could\n    94\tnever provide, because a hold only ever *prevented* something.\n    95\t\n    96\tThree rules still hold, because they are about the file's shape:\n    97\t\n    98\t1. **Write it before the load.** `status` should report what the batch is\n    99\t   about to have, not what it had a moment ago.\n   100\t2. **The first line is the one that matters.** In a multi-wave batch the\n    55\t   constraint and choosing on window size alone picks the wrong model.\n    56\t   Context decides only when the repo or transcript actually approaches it.\n    57\t\n    58\t## Topology on two boxes\n    59\t\n    60\tTwo boxes are two independent generation streams, and each is still\n    61\tsingle-stream internally. The shape that uses both:\n    62\t\n    63\t1. **Plan phase** on the flex box (the one that does not declare\n    64\t   `keep_resident` for the generalist) — it is expected to be swapped.\n    65\t2. **Run phase**: the manager stays on the box that already holds the\n    66\t   generalist, so it needs no swap and survives reboots if restore is enabled;\n    67\t   workers go to the flex box.\n    68\t\n    69\tTwo consequences worth stating out loud before the run:\n    70\t\n    71\t- The plan phase and the workers share the flex box, so they are **sequential**.\n    72\t  Finish planning before loading a worker model, or the worker load evicts the\n    73\t  planner mid-thought.\n    74\t- Loading a worker on the flex box evicts that box's resident planner; a\n    75\t  manager that retries a failed turn onto the flex box evicts it too. See the\n    76\t  mesh-policy pitfall in SKILL.md.\n    77\t\n    78\t## Reporting it to the user\n    79\t\n    80\tGive the pick, the box, the NPU share and the reasoning setting in a table,\n    81\tthen one line each on *why that model* and *what to avoid*. Name the\n    82\tsolo-occupant and the dormant-orchestration cases explicitly — they are the\n    83\tchoices a reader would otherwise make confidently and wrongly.\n     1\t# Two-box capability difference matrix (read-only, verified 2026-11-13)\n     2\t\n     3\tProcedure for answering \"which box do I ask for this?\" across the two Tiiny\n     4\tappliances. Produces a per-model capability classification and a per-box\n     5\tsummary from **live device metadata only**.\n     6\t\n     7\t## The two boxes\n     8\t\n     9\t| | TinyAnt (vault) | TiinyOcto (flex) |\n    10\t|---|---|---|\n    11\t| IP | 198.51.100.198 | 198.51.100.192 |\n    12\t| Device ID | TNYM…redacted | TNYM…redacted |\n    13\t| Key env var | `TIINY_API_KEY` | `TIINY_API_KEY_2` |\n    14\t\n    15\tBoth carry the **same 93-model store** — identical model ids, and identical\n    16\t`npu_usage` / `context_window` / `type` / `capabilities` / `params` for every\n    17\tone of the 93. They differ **only in download state and residency**. Verified\n    18\tacross all 93 ids on 2026-11-13.\n    19\t\n    20\t## The endpoints that matter\n    21\t\n    22\t| Endpoint | Returns | Notes |\n    23\t|---|---|---|\n    24\t| `GET /api/v1/models/online_models` | full 93-model catalogue | **The store truth.** Carries `status`, `progress`, `npu_usage`, `context_window`, `type`, `capabilities`, `supported`, `input`, `output`, `params`, `desc`, `thinking`, `reasoning_levels` |\n    25\t| `GET /api/v1/models/running` | running + pending + instances | **The residency truth.** Each running instance carries its own `npu_usage` |\n    26\t| `GET /api/v1/npu/status` | one device: `model`, `mem_total_mb`, `mem_used_mb` | The **only** endpoint that gives the NPU budget |\n    27\t| `GET /api/v1/sys/status` -> `npus[]` | live working set | **Not the budget** — see below |\n    28\t| `GET /v1/models` | resident models only | Alias `default`; matches the store's `running` entries |\n    29\t\n    30\t`/v1/models` on the box lists only **resident** models. `/api/v1/models` lists\n   125\tSo the generalist never requires choosing a box; the choice only matters for\n   126\tspecialists, and TiinyOcto covers every category TinyAnt does.\n   127\t\n   128\t## Driving both boxes at once\n   129\t\n   130\tThe two boxes are separate hosts with separate NPUs, so **both can be managed\n   131\tconcurrently** — but only over HTTP. The paths differ and the difference costs\n   132\tan afternoon if it is discovered mid-sprint.\n   133\t\n   134\t| Path | Both boxes at once? | Why |\n   135\t|---|---|---|\n   136\t| `POST /mesh/lifecycle` `{action, model, target}` on the mesh gateway | **yes** | one call per target, gateway key, reaches each box over its own bearer, needs no CLI session |\n   137\t| `POST /api/v1/models/<id>/start\\|stop` on the box, with that box's bearer | **yes** | what the lifecycle route does underneath; no session needed |\n   138\t| `tiiny_ctl.py load/unload` | **no** | `device_key()` reads only `TIINY_API_KEY`, `MANAGEMENT_BASE` only `TIINY_HOST`, and both shell out to `tiiny load`, whose session is bound to one device |\n   139\t| `tiiny load` by hand | **no** | signing in for the second box signs the first out |\n   140\t\n   141\t**Driving the second box with the controller** — per-box env in that shell\n   142\tonly; the four state files matter, or the two controllers contend for one lock:\n   143\t\n   144\t```bash\n   145\tTIINY_HOST=http://198.51.100.192 \\\n   146\tTIINY_API_KEY=\"$TIINY_API_KEY_2\" \\\n   147\tTIINY_LOCK_FILE=~/.hermes/tiiny-octo-lock.json \\\n   148\tTIINY_PLAN_FILE=~/.hermes/tiiny-octo-plan.json \\\n   149\tTIINY_HOLD_FILE=~/.hermes/tiiny-octo-hold \\\n   150\tTIINY_SNAPSHOT_FILE=~/.hermes/tiiny-octo-snapshot.json \\\n   151\t  python3 scripts/tiiny_ctl.py status\n   152\t```\n   153\t\n   154\t**Concurrent load, verified** — start the same model on both boxes from two\n   155\tthreads, then read residency back from each device:\n   156\t\n   157\t```python\n   158\timport json, threading, urllib.request\n   159\tkey = open(os.path.expanduser(\"~/.hermes/mesh/gateway_key\")).read().strip()\n   160\tdef post(target):\n   161\t    body = json.dumps({\"action\": \"start\", \"model\": MODEL, \"target\": target}).encode()\n   162\t    r = urllib.request.Request(\"http://127.0.0.1:8767/mesh/lifecycle\", data=body,\n   163\t        headers={\"Authorization\": f\"Bearer {key}\", \"Content-Type\": \"application/json\"},\n   164\t        method=\"POST\")\n   165\t    urllib.request.urlopen(r, timeout=120)\n   166\t```\n   167\t\n   168\tBoth answers are `ok` and both models land; the incumbents survive because the\n   169\trequested model fit the free share. Undo with the same call and `action: stop`\n   170\t— always leave the residency you found.\n   171\t\n   172\t**The two boxes do not agree on error shape, so never judge by the response\n   173\tcode.** Stopping a model that was not resident returned HTTP 500\n   174\t`Internal Server Error` on one box and `ok: true` with\n   175\t`stop_reason: \"explicit_stop\"` on the other, for the same request. Read\n   176\t`GET /api/v1/models/running` on the box and treat a 500 from a non-resident\n   177\tstop as \"nothing to stop\", not a failed unload.\n   178\t\n   179\t**Two boxes means two waves in flight, not two children per box.** Each box is\n   180\tstill single-stream, so a four-wave plan runs as two concurrent lanes of two\n   181\tsequential waves.\n   182\t\n   183\t## Device-side data bugs found\n   184\t\n   185\t1. `TaoBaiBai2025/Z-Image-Turbo-Creating-Realistic` carries the\n   186\t   **CharacterDesignSheet** description verbatim — the catalogue's own\n   187\t   copy-paste error, and it picks the wrong image model if you trust the desc.\n   188\t2. `Qwen/Qwen-Image-2.1-PE-T2I` is typed `Text Generation` but is really an\n   189\t   image prompt expander (`supported: ['Prompt Enhance']`).\n   190\t3. Two Octo-ready models carry `need_update` with no `update_title`:\n   191\t   `Qwen/Qwen-Image-2.1-PE-T2I`, `openai/Whisper-large-v3-turbo`.\n   192\t\n   193\t## Reproducible script\n   194\t\n   195\t`scripts/tiiny_two_box_matrix.py` (in this skill directory) runs the whole\n   196\tread-only pass: fetches both catalogues, classifies every model with the\n   197\tevidence recorded, writes the markdown matrix, and re-reads both devices to\n   198\tverify every headline number before exiting non-zero on any mismatch.\n   199\t\n   200\t```bash\n   201\tpython3 scripts/tiiny_two_box_matrix.py            # writes the matrix\n   202\tpython3 scripts/tiiny_two_box_matrix.py --verify   # verify an existing matrix\n   203\t```\n   204\t\n   205\tIt never loads, unloads, starts, stops or downloads anything. It reads\n   206\t`~/.hermes/.env` for the two key env vars by name and never prints a value.","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01NgCyCj7QehaHmZKjBaMhFu","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
