{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01DW4GpEi7BQbJCC2TPgutZ2","type":"tool_result","content":"**The device serves one generation-heavy stream at a time.** Two heavy\nchildren starve each other, and the failure mode is a *hang*, not an error:\nthe device keeps reporting healthy while it answers nothing. Run one heavy\nchild per wave. A heavy device child and the parent's own cloud stream are\ntwo providers, so they genuinely run in parallel.\n\n**The watchdog fights you.** A device that reloads its required models every\nfew minutes will evict the research model you just loaded for a long task,\nmid-task. The fix is a hold file: while it exists, the watchdog reloads\nnothing. The hold check has to run *before* the cold-check — put it after,\nand a hold reads as \"cold\" and the reload evicts your model anyway.\n\n**Restore what you displaced.** A delegated batch that swapped in a\nspecialist should put the required set back when it finishes, or the next\nbatch inherits a device nobody configured.\n\nAll of that is handled by a controller:\n\n```bash\ntiiny_ctl.py plan --owner \"$AGENT\" --task \"what this batch is for\" \\\n  --goal \"index every file under the vault and classify each\"   # pick, fit, wave\ntiiny_ctl.py reserve --owner \"$AGENT\" --task \"what this batch is for\"  # lock it\ntiiny_ctl.py load --wave 1                                       # load, evict, verify\ntiiny_ctl.py release                                             # restore, unlock\n```\n\nThe plan writes the hold *before* the load, which closes the window where\nthe watchdog fires between the load and the hold, and the release verifies\nthe restore instead of assuming it.\n\n### Two agents, one device\n\nThe controller also holds the device against other agents, because two\ndelegations at once will fight over the same models and the same silicon.\nThe lock is a file taken under `flock`, so two agents cannot both believe\nthey hold it, and it **expires**, so a crashed agent does not lock the\ndevice overnight. Alongside it, a marked notice in the team's shared status\nboard tells agents that are not running this code that the device is spoken\nfor.\n\n### The image bonus\n\nThe same device runs the image models, and the same controller picks the\nright one for the job — a text-accurate default for a logo, a\nhigh-fidelity model for detail that IS the subject, a character sheet for a\nturnaround, an adapter for cutting a subject out of an existing photo. It\nis the kind of thing that looks like a bonus and turns out to be the reason\nthe image pipeline works at all.\n\n## What the reader gets\n\nThe routing table, the backend, the swap script, and a checker that shows\nwhat any given goal would be routed to:\n\n```bash\npython3 scripts/route_check.py \"index every file under the vault and classify each\"\n```\n\n```\ngoal:   index every file under the vault and classify each\ntier:   index  (inferred)\nmodel:  openai/gpt-oss-20b\nbudget: 27% NPU, 131072 context\nreasoning: levels only (low, medium, high) — the on/off switch is ignored\nthinking: auto (the model's native behaviour)\nresidency: loaded\n```\n\nAnd with a reasoning mode asked for:\n\n```bash\npython3 scripts/route_check.py --thinking off \"index every file under the vault\"\n```\n\n```\nthinking: off -> off→low\nsent:    enable_thinking=false, reasoning_effort=low, max_tokens=4096\n```\n\nThe checker reads the device's resident set — one read-only call to the local\nCLI — and the device's own capability for the model it picked. It changes\nnothing on the device. It exists so a routing decision can be checked\n*before* a batch spends tokens on it.\n\n## The limits\n\n**The routing table is device-specific.** The tier choices, NPU shares, and\ncontext windows here were read from one device's catalogue endpoint on\n2026-11-09, when the store held 92 models. The store changes with every\nTiinyOS update, and the NPU shares have already moved once — a model that\ntook 55% of the pool took 47% on the read behind this article. **Re-read the\nnumbers before trusting them.**\n\n**The reasoning capability is read at route time, so it is never stale in\nthe route.** The same is not true of this article's prose: the model's\ncapability table moves with the Model Store, and the route reads the\ndevice's own copy rather than any table shipped with it.\n\n**Reading the catalogue's description fields is not the same as reading the\nmodel's card.** The device's `desc` is the publisher's own summary and is\nreliable about specialisation. It is not a benchmark. Where this article\nnames a trait, that trait is quoted from the device's description, not\ninferred from a benchmark table.\n\n**Routing by shape is a heuristic.** It works because the models genuinely\nspecialise, and it fails when a task's real shape is not in its goal text.\nThe `tier` override exists for that.\n\n**This does not make the models interchangeable.** A better model on the\nright task is better; a worse model on the wrong task is not rescued by\nrouting. The table is a floor, not a ceiling.\n\n## Where to go next\n\n- [model-catalogue.md](../Tiiny/model-catalogue.md) — every model in the store and\n  what the device's own descriptions say it is good at.\n- [PATTERN.md](./PATTERN.md) — the routing pattern in full: the failure\n  modes, the tier design, and the reference checklist.\n- [IMPLEMENTATION.md](./IMPLEMENTATION.md) — the backend plugin, the swap\n  script, and an end-to-end setup checklist.\n- [scripts/route_check.py](./scripts/route_check.py) — the routing checker.\n\n`__init__.py` holds the provider class and a `register(ctx)` function that\ncalls `ctx.register_delegation_provider(...)`.\n\n`tiers.py` holds the tier table, the shape signals, and the special-model\ntable — as **pure data and pure functions**, with no Hermes imports, no\nnetwork calls and no device state.\n\n`thinking.py` holds the reasoning-mode control. It does read the device, but\nit does not import Hermes — so the standalone checker can use it too.\n\n**Keep them in their own files.** The plugin and the standalone scripts (the\ncontroller, the checker) all need the same table, and a table copied into\nthree places is a table that will disagree with itself. It is loaded **by\npath**, not by package name, for a specific reason: the Hermes plugin loader\nexecutes `__init__.py` through `importlib.util.spec_from_file_location`,\nwhich does not establish package semantics, so a relative\n`from .tiers import ...` fails there — and the scripts run outside Hermes\nentirely, where `from agent...` is not importable at all. Loading the file\nby path works in both places.\n\nCopy the whole directory. A tree with `__init__.py` but no `tiers.py` raises\n`ImportError` at load, and the backend then reads as \"never installed\" rather\nthan \"installed wrong\".\n\n### The tier table\n\nEvery number in this table came from the device's catalogue endpoint. The\nNPU share decides what can be resident alongside what; the context window\ndecides what a child can hold.\n\n---\n\n## 2b. The device controller\n\nThe swap script is for a human at a terminal. A manager agent running a batch\nneeds the same things done for it, in order, without a human in the loop — and\nit needs the device kept away from other agents while it works.\n\n`scripts/tiiny_ctl.py` is that. Seven commands:\n\n```bash\ntiiny_ctl.py status                                # device, residency, lock, hold, plan\ntiiny_ctl.py plan --owner \"$AGENT\" --task \"...\" \\\n  --goal \"index every file under the vault\"       # pick, fit, wave\ntiiny_ctl.py reserve --owner \"$AGENT\" --task \"...\" # take the lock\ntiiny_ctl.py load --wave 1                         # evict, load, verify\ntiiny_ctl.py release                               # restore, unlock\ntiiny_ctl.py catalog --refresh                     # what moved in the store\ntiiny_ctl.py image \"a logo with the word ARC in it\"\n```\n\n\n---\n\n## 3. The routing checker\n\n`scripts/route_check.py` applies the same tier table and the same shape\ndetection, then prints the tier, the model, the budget and what the model\ncan do with reasoning — so a routing decision can be checked without\nspawning a child.\n\n```bash\npython3 scripts/route_check.py \"index every file under the vault and classify each\"\npython3 scripts/route_check.py --thinking off \"index every file under the vault\"\npython3 scripts/route_check.py --all\npython3 scripts/route_check.py --tier code --alternatives \"anything\"\n```\n\n```\ngoal:   index every file under the vault and classify each\ntier:   index  (inferred)\nmodel:  openai/gpt-oss-20b\nbudget: 27% NPU, 131072 context\nreasoning: levels only (low, medium, high) — the on/off switch is ignored\nthinking: off -> off→low\nsent:    enable_thinking=false, reasoning_effort=low, max_tokens=4096\nresidency: loaded\n```\n\nThe checker makes read-only calls — the device's resident model list, and the\ndevice's catalogue when a reasoning mode is asked for — and changes nothing\non the device. The residency line comes from `tiiny top`.\n\nIt finds the plugin's shared modules by path, in either layout the files\nship in, so the checker cannot drift from the backend it is checking.\n\n---\n\n## 4. End-to-end checklist\n\n- [ ] The bearer is in the environment as `TIINY_API_KEY`.\n- [ ] The `tiiny` CLI is on `PATH`, and `tiiny top` answers.\n- [ ] The `tiiny` provider is in `config.yaml`, with `key_env: TIINY_API_KEY`.\n- [ ] The backend plugin is installed and `delegation.backend` selects it.\n- [ ] The whole plugin directory was copied, not just `__init__.py`.\n- [ ] The tier table's NPU shares and context windows were read from the\n      device, not copied from an earlier read.\n- [ ] Every model in the tier table is in the store.\n- [ ] The fallback provider is configured, and its use is visible.\n- [ ] The controller can plan, reserve, load and release, and its release\n      restores the required set and verifies it.\n- [ ] The swap script is in place, and the watchdog honours the hold.\n- [ ] The hold check runs before the cold-check in the watchdog.\n- [ ] `route_check.py` returns the expected tier for a known goal.\n- [ ] `route_check.py --thinking off` reports the state the model would\n      reach, and it is the state the route label then reports.\n- [ ] A reasoning task is not run on a small output budget: the floor is set,\n      and raised together with the budget it belongs to.\n- [ ] A batch with mixed subtasks shows more than one model in its results.\n- [ ] After a deliberate swap, the required models are resident again.\n- [ ] **A plugin change is loaded, not just written.** Editing the plugin\n      file on disk changes nothing in a running gateway — Python has already\n      imported the module. Reload it, and reload it *for every profile that\n      runs it*, because a profile's delegation backend is resolved under that\n      profile's home.\n- [ ] The child's route says the provider you expect. A child that quietly\n      fell back to the paid provider still reports success.\nThe pattern has one non-obvious property: **the cheapest correct decision is\nusually not the best model, it is the smallest model that fits the task.**\nThat sounds obvious and is routinely violated, because the best model is the\none with the most impressive description and the routing decision defaults to\nit.\n\n### Route by shape, not by keyword\n\nTask shape is what predicts which model does well. A goal that says \"index\nthe vault\" is an indexing job regardless of what else it mentions. A research\ntask *about* code is still a research task — keyword matching gets that one\nwrong, because it finds \"code\" in the goal and routes to the coder.\n\nShape is not a keyword list either. It is the *operation* the task performs:\ngathering evidence, enumerating and labelling, editing files, driving a\nbrowser, producing prose. Those five operations are what the catalogue's","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"95803e3d-040d-4401-b98c-4ca7b716b706","uuid":"8318d003-f2ac-40ea-827b-8110ff8a5524","timestamp":"2026-11-21T06:01:05.395Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"**The device serves one generation-heavy stream at a time.** Two heavy\nchildren starve each other, and the failure mode is a *hang*, not an error:\nthe device keeps reporting healthy while it answers nothing. Run one heavy\nchild per wave. A heavy device child and the parent's own cloud stream are\ntwo providers, so they genuinely run in parallel.\n\n**The watchdog fights you.** A device that reloads its required models every\nfew minutes will evict the research model you just loaded for a long task,\nmid-task. The fix is a hold file: while it exists, the watchdog reloads\nnothing. The hold check has to run *before* the cold-check — put it after,\nand a hold reads as \"cold\" and the reload evicts your model anyway.\n\n**Restore what you displaced.** A delegated batch that swapped in a\nspecialist should put the required set back when it finishes, or the next\nbatch inherits a device nobody configured.\n\nAll of that is handled by a controller:\n\n```bash\ntiiny_ctl.py plan --owner \"$AGENT\" --task \"what this batch is for\" \\\n  --goal \"index every file under the vault and classify each\"   # pick, fit, wave\ntiiny_ctl.py reserve --owner \"$AGENT\" --task \"what this batch is for\"  # lock it\ntiiny_ctl.py load --wave 1                                       # load, evict, verify\ntiiny_ctl.py release                                             # restore, unlock\n```\n\nThe plan writes the hold *before* the load, which closes the window where\nthe watchdog fires between the load and the hold, and the release verifies\nthe restore instead of assuming it.\n\n### Two agents, one device\n\nThe controller also holds the device against other agents, because two\ndelegations at once will fight over the same models and the same silicon.\nThe lo